peratorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-3.54+2.2\\text{ }\\,\\theta -3\\text{ }\\,{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =2.2-6\\text{ }\\,\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.2-6\\text{ }\\,\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 6\\text{ }\\,\\theta  & =2.2 \\\\ \\theta  & ≈0.367\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}≈0.367,$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5a1816a3-cd64-5456-9517-95ae4181b629"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5a1816a3-cd64-5456-9517-95ae4181b629" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a1816a3-cd64-5456-9517-95ae4181b629" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-12790bd2-fdb6-5ae2-9339-61f0fb556461" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c15cc0bb-88d7-5a65-b0be-5fe61a791505" "answer-e0b77137-f026-54f7-b0e8-585ac5b704af" "answer-56d327c3-e05f-5754-88f5-dbc857c6043f" "answer-12790bd2-fdb6-5ae2-9339-61f0fb556461" "answer-1127dce4-9c1c-5edb-917f-38707912afec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c15cc0bb-88d7-5a65-b0be-5fe61a791505" :answer/id #uuid "c15cc0bb-88d7-5a65-b0be-5fe61a791505" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.065" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0b77137-f026-54f7-b0e8-585ac5b704af" :answer/id #uuid "e0b77137-f026-54f7-b0e8-585ac5b704af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.112" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56d327c3-e05f-5754-88f5-dbc857c6043f" :answer/id #uuid "56d327c3-e05f-5754-88f5-dbc857c6043f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.204" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12790bd2-fdb6-5ae2-9339-61f0fb556461" :answer/id #uuid "12790bd2-fdb6-5ae2-9339-61f0fb556461" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.367" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1127dce4-9c1c-5edb-917f-38707912afec" :answer/id #uuid "1127dce4-9c1c-5edb-917f-38707912afec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.073" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d69af2de-f48e-5047-98b1-6977b8b554d5" :question/id #uuid "d69af2de-f48e-5047-98b1-6977b8b554d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-159940" :question/problem "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent exponential random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -7.8\\text{ }\\,\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -7.8\\text{ }\\,\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\theta }-7.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\theta }-7.8 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\theta } & =7.8 \\\\ 4 & =7.8\\text{ }\\,\\theta  \\\\ \\theta  & ≈0.513\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}≈0.513,$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f0558f9a-6e48-5095-9d94-0109cf7402ad"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f0558f9a-6e48-5095-9d94-0109cf7402ad" :answer-field/id #uuid "f0558f9a-6e48-5095-9d94-0109cf7402ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f91e9e3e-0341-5556-a0b5-5691bcc58340" :answer-field/choices ["answer-da20a45a-b8d5-564b-b608-5e3791c34ad2" "answer-f91e9e3e-0341-5556-a0b5-5691bcc58340" "answer-67ea16bb-5fc8-5966-91a6-89f6143bb573" "answer-3de46f7c-9501-5c63-9068-1ec131a5e103" "answer-01b7e61a-87f8-5dfb-b095-ddd7b27158f1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da20a45a-b8d5-564b-b608-5e3791c34ad2" :answer/id #uuid "da20a45a-b8d5-564b-b608-5e3791c34ad2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.458" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f91e9e3e-0341-5556-a0b5-5691bcc58340" :answer/id #uuid "f91e9e3e-0341-5556-a0b5-5691bcc58340" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.513" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67ea16bb-5fc8-5966-91a6-89f6143bb573" :answer/id #uuid "67ea16bb-5fc8-5966-91a6-89f6143bb573" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.617" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3de46f7c-9501-5c63-9068-1ec131a5e103" :answer/id #uuid "3de46f7c-9501-5c63-9068-1ec131a5e103" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.734" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-01b7e61a-87f8-5dfb-b095-ddd7b27158f1" :answer/id #uuid "01b7e61a-87f8-5dfb-b095-ddd7b27158f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.765" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-54d12174-c052-5371-9f75-2200e071a1fb" :question/id #uuid "54d12174-c052-5371-9f75-2200e071a1fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-159945" :question/problem "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent normal random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-9.6+12.6\\text{ }\\,\\theta -8\\text{ }\\,{\\theta }^{2},\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(-8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-9.6+12.6\\text{ }\\,\\theta -8\\text{ }\\,{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =12.6-16\\text{ }\\,\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12.6-16\\text{ }\\,\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 16\\text{ }\\,\\theta  & =12.6 \\\\ \\theta  & ≈0.788\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}≈0.788,$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0e02fd70-4bfa-5ee0-87ea-68ee321a3054"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0e02fd70-4bfa-5ee0-87ea-68ee321a3054" :answer-field/id #uuid "0e02fd70-4bfa-5ee0-87ea-68ee321a3054" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7ae6bb66-be05-5dcb-8863-b23c52551053" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c3d0c384-6c7e-5b36-a4e1-184863f1c6e7" "answer-7ae6bb66-be05-5dcb-8863-b23c52551053" "answer-f18f76ba-32bc-57a4-ac9a-52ff4682af02" "answer-fa0b1bf7-e11d-54c6-a696-04e144a0cba6" "answer-23fcd9a5-0cf5-5d2e-97e7-871a82a5410c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3d0c384-6c7e-5b36-a4e1-184863f1c6e7" :answer/id #uuid "c3d0c384-6c7e-5b36-a4e1-184863f1c6e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.778" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ae6bb66-be05-5dcb-8863-b23c52551053" :answer/id #uuid "7ae6bb66-be05-5dcb-8863-b23c52551053" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.788" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f18f76ba-32bc-57a4-ac9a-52ff4682af02" :answer/id #uuid "f18f76ba-32bc-57a4-ac9a-52ff4682af02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.817" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa0b1bf7-e11d-54c6-a696-04e144a0cba6" :answer/id #uuid "fa0b1bf7-e11d-54c6-a696-04e144a0cba6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.878" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23fcd9a5-0cf5-5d2e-97e7-871a82a5410c" :answer/id #uuid "23fcd9a5-0cf5-5d2e-97e7-871a82a5410c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.887" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-31869bf1-d8be-5a63-a737-e3c2335ce819" :question/id #uuid "31869bf1-d8be-5a63-a737-e3c2335ce819" :question/math-academy-id "q-159941" :question/problem "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent normal random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-8.4+7.4\\text{ }\\,\\theta -6\\text{ }\\,{\\theta }^{2},\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-8.4+7.4\\text{ }\\,\\theta -6\\text{ }\\,{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =7.4-12\\text{ }\\,\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7.4-12\\text{ }\\,\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 12\\text{ }\\,\\theta  & =7.4 \\\\ \\theta  & ≈0.617\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}≈0.617,$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-30f99177-a32e-55ae-845f-bfa34cf66810"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-30f99177-a32e-55ae-845f-bfa34cf66810" :answer-field/id #uuid "30f99177-a32e-55ae-845f-bfa34cf66810" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-22e80040-d18e-5faa-af39-593a97881279" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4d8bb5ed-e27a-5e79-a56d-335ca515db9c" "answer-22e80040-d18e-5faa-af39-593a97881279" "answer-6f07d653-9ff4-5a87-a55f-d3592fa58cf3" "answer-d50a9899-221a-5f45-912c-5d49f82f44f6" "answer-18079953-7a0d-57a4-8843-c654a6bb39fe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d8bb5ed-e27a-5e79-a56d-335ca515db9c" :answer/id #uuid "4d8bb5ed-e27a-5e79-a56d-335ca515db9c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.581" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22e80040-d18e-5faa-af39-593a97881279" :answer/id #uuid "22e80040-d18e-5faa-af39-593a97881279" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.617" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f07d653-9ff4-5a87-a55f-d3592fa58cf3" :answer/id #uuid "6f07d653-9ff4-5a87-a55f-d3592fa58cf3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.714" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d50a9899-221a-5f45-912c-5d49f82f44f6" :answer/id #uuid "d50a9899-221a-5f45-912c-5d49f82f44f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.819" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18079953-7a0d-57a4-8843-c654a6bb39fe" :answer/id #uuid "18079953-7a0d-57a4-8843-c654a6bb39fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.891" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4510ff72-383e-5b2d-aa54-f6d78aa69e2d" :question/id #uuid "4510ff72-383e-5b2d-aa54-f6d78aa69e2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-159939" :question/problem "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent exponential random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -15.8\\text{ }\\,\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -15.8\\text{ }\\,\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\theta }-15.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\theta }-15.8 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\theta } & =15.8 \\\\ 5 & =15.8\\text{ }\\,\\theta  \\\\ \\theta  & ≈0.316\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}≈0.316,$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-34a1e506-0c01-548c-8aeb-dbb4e0856d79"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-34a1e506-0c01-548c-8aeb-dbb4e0856d79" :answer-field/id #uuid "34a1e506-0c01-548c-8aeb-dbb4e0856d79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cf461d27-016b-5aa4-a77d-15596b89f756" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5562092f-3fe0-5bcf-8339-c7daec6026f2" "answer-56b4bf5e-9806-5a09-a3b3-65589f4e73ef" "answer-8bedb81d-563d-5133-aea2-1add3162bcde" "answer-cf461d27-016b-5aa4-a77d-15596b89f756" "answer-aa8e2520-683e-5f18-9745-b1b59e87e0a4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5562092f-3fe0-5bcf-8339-c7daec6026f2" :answer/id #uuid "5562092f-3fe0-5bcf-8339-c7daec6026f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.208" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56b4bf5e-9806-5a09-a3b3-65589f4e73ef" :answer/id #uuid "56b4bf5e-9806-5a09-a3b3-65589f4e73ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.213" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8bedb81d-563d-5133-aea2-1add3162bcde" :answer/id #uuid "8bedb81d-563d-5133-aea2-1add3162bcde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.276" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf461d27-016b-5aa4-a77d-15596b89f756" :answer/id #uuid "cf461d27-016b-5aa4-a77d-15596b89f756" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.316" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa8e2520-683e-5f18-9745-b1b59e87e0a4" :answer/id #uuid "aa8e2520-683e-5f18-9745-b1b59e87e0a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.412" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163265 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a random sample $X_1, X_2, X_3$ drawn from a population where $X_i\\sim B(5,\\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown probability of success. Let's assume we conducted our sample and got the following data:\n\n$$\nx_1 = 4, \\qquad x_2 = 1, \\qquad x_3 = 1\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that the log-likelihood function for this sample is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta) = \\ln  125 + 6 \\ln \\theta +9\\ln(1-\\theta).\n$$\n\nOur goal is to form an estimate for the unknown parameter $\\theta.$ To do this, we find the value of $\\theta$ that maximizes the probability of getting the observed sample data. In other words, we need to maximize the likelihood function $L(\\theta).$ And, since the natural logarithm is an increasing function, a value of $\\theta$ that maximizes the likelihood function $L(\\theta)$ will also maximize the log-likelihood function $l(\\theta).$\n\nEstimates of an unknown parameter $\\theta$ obtained by maximizing the likelihood function (or, equivalently, the log-likelihood function) are called **maximum likelihood estimates**. We'll use the usual $\\hat{\\theta}$ notation to denote a maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta.$ \n\n![](images/00/005367bc78937c5bd5b684cb260aff951de2e85d7c2907bb6e11c312ad4f24bd.png)\n\nTo find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta.$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡125+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\theta }+\\frac{9}{1-\\theta }\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\theta }+\\frac{9}{\\theta -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{\\theta }+\\frac{9}{\\theta -1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{6(\\theta -1)+9\\theta }{\\theta (\\theta -1)} & =0 \\\\ 6(\\theta -1)+9\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 15\\theta -6 & =0 \\\\ 15\\theta  & =6 \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{6}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$ is $\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,} =  \\dfrac{6}{15}.$ \n\n**Note**: It's easy to check that this is a maximum of $l(\\theta)$ by calculating the second derivative. We'll omit this step." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163361 :tutorial/content "Let's derive a general expression for the maximum likelihood estimator for the unknown parameter $\\theta$ for a set of $n$ independent and identically distributed Bernoulli random variables.\n\nSuppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{n}$ is an I.I.D random sample, where $X_i\\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta)$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta.$ For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{n},$ the log-likelihood function is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta) = S\\ln\\theta + (n-S)\\ln(1-\\theta),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\displaystyle S = \\sum_ {i=1}^n x_i$ is the number of successes in the sample. \n\nTo compute a general expression for the maximum likelihood estimate, we differentiate $l(\\theta),$ set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta.$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\frac{S}{\\theta }+\\frac{n-S}{1-\\theta }\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{S}{\\theta }+\\frac{n-S}{\\theta -1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{S}{\\theta }+\\frac{n-S}{\\theta -1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{S(\\theta -1)+(n-S)\\theta }{\\theta (\\theta -1)} & =0 \\\\ S(\\theta -1)+(n-S)\\theta  & =0 \\\\ \\cancel{S\\theta }-S+n\\theta -\\cancel{S\\theta } & =0 \\\\ n\\theta -S & =0 \\\\ n\\theta  & =S \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{S}{n} \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$ is \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,} =  \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_ {i=1}^n x_i,\n$$\n\nwhich is simply the *mean of the sample*. \n\nThe maximum likelihood estimate for other common distributions can be derived similarly. Let's summarize the most important cases." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163362 :tutorial/content "The table below summarizes the maximum likelihood estimates for several common probability distributions. These results can be derived by maximizing the corresponding log-likelihood function. \n\n| **Distribution** | **Probability Mass Function, $f(x; \\theta)$** | **Parameter, $\\theta$** | **Maximum Likelihood Estimate, $\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Bernoulli | $\\displaystyle \\theta^x(1-\\theta)^{1-x}$ | The probability of success | $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i$ \n The mean of the sample |\n| Binomial | $\\displaystyle {N \\choose x} \\theta^x (1-\\theta)^{N-x}$ | The probability of success | $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i$ \n The mean of the sample |\n| Poisson | $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\theta^{x} e^{-\\theta}}{x!}$ | The rate parameter | $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i$ \n The mean of the sample |\n| Geometric | $\\displaystyle (1-\\theta)^{x-1} \\theta$ | The probability of success | $\\displaystyle \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i \\bigg)^{-1}$ \n The reciprocal of the sample mean |\n| Exponential | $\\displaystyle \\theta e^{-\\theta x}$ | The rate parameter | $\\displaystyle \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i \\bigg)^{-1}$ \n The reciprocal of the sample mean |\n| Normal | $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{\\theta_2 \\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-\\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{x-\\theta_1}{\\theta_2} \\right)^2}$ | The mean $(\\theta_1)$ \n\n The standard deviation $(\\theta_2)$ | $\\displaystyle \\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}_1 = \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i$ \n The mean of the sample \n\n $\\displaystyle \\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}_2 = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})^2}$ \n The standard deviation of the sample |" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162205 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186114517 :answer-field/choices 17592186114518] [:db/retract 17592186114517 :answer-field/choices 17592186114519] [:db/retract 17592186114517 :answer-field/choices 17592186114520] [:db/retract 17592186114517 :answer-field/choices 17592186114521] [:db/retract 17592186114517 :answer-field/choices 17592186114522] {:db/id 17592186115267 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4aa71abb-1f45-5c68-95b9-e97d2bf13015" "question-3f99401c-4f2a-57bb-bccb-f67190e5a3b3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115096 :question/problem "Which of the following plots is the graph of $y = -x^3?$\n\n![](images/ba/babbe4306b4a068c96f9dc820af3b02a6e25d5779928108f3d783dc0684fdf27.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, remember that for $x>0,$ the graph of $y=-x^3$ decreases very rapidly as we increase $x.$ We say that $y \\rightarrow -\\infty$ as $x \\rightarrow \\infty.$ The only graphs that exhibit this decreasing property are III and IV.\n\nNow, we need to choose between graphs III and IV. To do this, let's evaluate $y=-x^3$ at some value of $x,$ say $x=1.$ Substituting $x=1$ into the function, we get\n\n$$\ny = -(1)^3 = -1,\n$$\n\nso the correct graph must pass through the point $(1,-1).$ Only graph III satisfies this criterion.\n\nTherefore, graph III corresponds to the graph of $y=-x^3.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115268 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d93e70df-0d5a-518e-ab33-73b00d22f653" "question-d3b4690a-a388-56c8-b51f-66b065fce082" "question-51849fa8-ba8b-5524-9925-28a75279e347"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115097 :question/problem "Sketch the graph of $y=x^3+2.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we have $y=x^3{\\color{blue}\\,+\\,2},$ we need to translate the graph of $y=x^3$ *up* by $\\color{blue}2$ units. \n\nSo, the graph of $y=x^3+2$ looks as follows:\n\n![](images/54/54030b86feaed3fd9b3e818654dae831f4456159d6ae32f5e3619b6f0e1369a7.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115269 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-818b8208-c98b-5ec3-a330-6bbba9ff0a1a" "question-128afa31-6089-55bb-b4c5-d34347e7a11c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115098 :question/problem "Sketch the graph of $y=(x-2)^3.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we have $\\color{blue}-2$ inside the brackets, we translate the graph of $y=x^3$ by $\\color{blue}2$ units to the *right*. \n\nSo, the graph of $y=(x-2)^3$ looks as follows:\n\n![](images/82/82978268bdfe405201f3b5e3e8927c81c3379bcf2933a34e15b6ba304cbd770d.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115270 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-26a8d25b-e6dd-5460-9414-80558f8bec9d" "question-2b8be0e4-6ba2-573c-9c5f-c0fd730c101c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115099 :question/problem "Plot the graph of $y=-(x+1)^3-3.$" :question/worked-solution "The given function can be obtained by translating $y=-x^3$ both horizontally and vertically.\n\nWe have two translations:\n\n- the $\\color{red}+1$ inside the parentheses means a horizontal translation of $1$ unit to the *left*, and\n\n- the $\\color{blue}-3$ outside the parentheses means a vertical translation of $3$ units *down*.\n\nSo, the new graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/b5/b5f3fac4f76321abdd825c6e7d5ab5b60916149bbffc7a94a8bd3e932c17d8ea.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-4aa71abb-1f45-5c68-95b9-e97d2bf13015" :question/id #uuid "4aa71abb-1f45-5c68-95b9-e97d2bf13015" :question/math-academy-id "q-111955" :question/problem "![](images/05/05173f7d9b4cdf89d4167f71533da6b95942bf6355bc6245fc95fbd7c434440e.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above show the graph of $y=-{x}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that graph III resembles a straight line. So, we disregard this option.\n\nNow, remember that for $x>0,$ the graph of $y=-{x}^{3}$ decreases very rapidly as we increase $x.$ We say that $y\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ The only graphs that exhibit this decreasing property are I and II.\n\nNow, we need to choose between graphs I and II. To do this, let's evaluate $y=-{x}^{3}$ at some value of $x,$ say $x=0.$ Substituting $x=0$ into the function, we get\n\n$$\ny=-(0)^{3}=0,\n$$\n\nso the correct graph must pass through the point $(0,0).$ Only graph II satisfies this criterion.\n\nTherefore, graph II corresponds to the graph of $y=-{x}^{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c1a37a95-3f08-5505-9824-80e83cc36021"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c1a37a95-3f08-5505-9824-80e83cc36021" :answer-field/id #uuid "c1a37a95-3f08-5505-9824-80e83cc36021" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-82ee4a4f-63b3-5299-b797-c0c7b0604298" :answer-field/choices ["answer-33fea308-610c-5421-ab79-bb3a1649e672" "answer-82ee4a4f-63b3-5299-b797-c0c7b0604298" "answer-6ce8035c-cf2a-5f2a-b299-14745d4be4c7" "answer-d9f3a87e-a8b6-506d-a966-786618c6b1cc" "answer-7670b248-dbe6-5812-a098-f64191a82dee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33fea308-610c-5421-ab79-bb3a1649e672" :answer/id #uuid "33fea308-610c-5421-ab79-bb3a1649e672" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82ee4a4f-63b3-5299-b797-c0c7b0604298" :answer/id #uuid "82ee4a4f-63b3-5299-b797-c0c7b0604298" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ce8035c-cf2a-5f2a-b299-14745d4be4c7" :answer/id #uuid "6ce8035c-cf2a-5f2a-b299-14745d4be4c7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9f3a87e-a8b6-506d-a966-786618c6b1cc" :answer/id #uuid "d9f3a87e-a8b6-506d-a966-786618c6b1cc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7670b248-dbe6-5812-a098-f64191a82dee" :answer/id #uuid "7670b248-dbe6-5812-a098-f64191a82dee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3f99401c-4f2a-57bb-bccb-f67190e5a3b3" :question/id #uuid "3f99401c-4f2a-57bb-bccb-f67190e5a3b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-28208" :question/problem "![](images/7d/7d341093cf35425a75174a6871d452c38daa0d281cdc2a2b7aaceeca390dee5b.png)\n\nWhich of the following curves is the graph of $y={x}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, remember that for $x>0,$ the graph of $y={x}^{3}$ increases very rapidly as we increase $x.$ We say that $y\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ The only graphs that exhibit this increasing property are II and IV.\n\nNow, we need to choose between graphs II and IV. To do this, let's evaluate $y={x}^{3}$ at some value of $x,$ say $x=-1.$ Substituting $x=-1$ into the function, we get\n\n$$\ny=(-1)^{3}=-1,\n$$\n\nso the correct graph must pass through the point $(-1,-1).$ Only graph IV satisfies this criterion.\n\nTherefore, graph IV corresponds to the graph of $y={x}^{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ddbcedd3-6d01-50a2-b5d0-e40ab1637157"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ddbcedd3-6d01-50a2-b5d0-e40ab1637157" :answer-field/id #uuid "ddbcedd3-6d01-50a2-b5d0-e40ab1637157" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-fiel