on/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9dbce107-591f-5d85-812c-95ee8edfbc6a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9dbce107-591f-5d85-812c-95ee8edfbc6a" :answer-field/id #uuid "9dbce107-591f-5d85-812c-95ee8edfbc6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d80c98bf-58f9-5e87-ad56-b99917815c6c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0749b2da-1771-5909-bbe1-4880efe69f06" "answer-3969f079-4a44-51f9-887b-493ad558b063" "answer-3da5e75e-fb2e-5c5b-9792-b84d846d7722" "answer-5476f41d-a4df-5371-9cf6-0362dff06a50" "answer-d80c98bf-58f9-5e87-ad56-b99917815c6c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0749b2da-1771-5909-bbe1-4880efe69f06" :answer/id #uuid "0749b2da-1771-5909-bbe1-4880efe69f06" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3969f079-4a44-51f9-887b-493ad558b063" :answer/id #uuid "3969f079-4a44-51f9-887b-493ad558b063" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3da5e75e-fb2e-5c5b-9792-b84d846d7722" :answer/id #uuid "3da5e75e-fb2e-5c5b-9792-b84d846d7722" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5476f41d-a4df-5371-9cf6-0362dff06a50" :answer/id #uuid "5476f41d-a4df-5371-9cf6-0362dff06a50" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d80c98bf-58f9-5e87-ad56-b99917815c6c" :answer/id #uuid "d80c98bf-58f9-5e87-ad56-b99917815c6c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-98e4ebcc-8114-515c-b8a9-5feafd8f7bb3" :question/id #uuid "98e4ebcc-8114-515c-b8a9-5feafd8f7bb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-102967" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}?$\n\nI. $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty $\n\nII. The range of $f(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,0]$\n\nIII. The graph of $f(x)$ lies in the first and third quadrants" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n![](images/ab/abafb7d55b4baa0b06f20c3de2b44d5925f7286f078aaa76d71aded7c9c9a001.png)\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is false since the range of $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the graph of $f(x)$ lies in the first and third quadrants.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-61b564d7-3dd3-5be8-b00c-7d4c0105b021"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-61b564d7-3dd3-5be8-b00c-7d4c0105b021" :answer-field/id #uuid "61b564d7-3dd3-5be8-b00c-7d4c0105b021" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1e8a624a-766c-5521-a7e0-68fa10357b2c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ff18e589-7302-5c48-9ca4-e66cc9290084" "answer-955053f1-ca40-507d-adfd-f164744f1aaa" "answer-e7dc61b4-da33-58f5-acc1-5cb4210241cf" "answer-46095390-0a49-5a60-94b9-d4b2808b83a9" "answer-1e8a624a-766c-5521-a7e0-68fa10357b2c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff18e589-7302-5c48-9ca4-e66cc9290084" :answer/id #uuid "ff18e589-7302-5c48-9ca4-e66cc9290084" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-955053f1-ca40-507d-adfd-f164744f1aaa" :answer/id #uuid "955053f1-ca40-507d-adfd-f164744f1aaa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7dc61b4-da33-58f5-acc1-5cb4210241cf" :answer/id #uuid "e7dc61b4-da33-58f5-acc1-5cb4210241cf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46095390-0a49-5a60-94b9-d4b2808b83a9" :answer/id #uuid "46095390-0a49-5a60-94b9-d4b2808b83a9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e8a624a-766c-5521-a7e0-68fa10357b2c" :answer/id #uuid "1e8a624a-766c-5521-a7e0-68fa10357b2c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-90b71102-b1fe-54a8-9326-1ad18a9d698b" :question/id #uuid "90b71102-b1fe-54a8-9326-1ad18a9d698b" :question/math-academy-id "q-89855" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}?$\n\nI. The range of $f(x)$ is $(-10,\\infty )$\n\nII. $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty $\n\nIII. The graph of $f(x)$ lies in the second and fourth quadrants" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n![](images/ab/abafb7d55b4baa0b06f20c3de2b44d5925f7286f078aaa76d71aded7c9c9a001.png)\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since the range of $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is false since the graph of $f(x)$ lies in the first and third quadrants\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f2080000-1873-59da-afbb-0f81bfffa11d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f2080000-1873-59da-afbb-0f81bfffa11d" :answer-field/id #uuid "f2080000-1873-59da-afbb-0f81bfffa11d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b60038db-477f-5ef7-9af4-1e5925f39ccc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d6785992-fea4-51e7-ba38-0c8c87bf84ee" "answer-532a1303-6f64-5e49-974c-c40278d75b29" "answer-52251806-d785-5838-a46d-8bf20af66d4a" "answer-b60038db-477f-5ef7-9af4-1e5925f39ccc" "answer-09fca4ca-1aba-590b-8f95-45dedb57d0bd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6785992-fea4-51e7-ba38-0c8c87bf84ee" :answer/id #uuid "d6785992-fea4-51e7-ba38-0c8c87bf84ee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-532a1303-6f64-5e49-974c-c40278d75b29" :answer/id #uuid "532a1303-6f64-5e49-974c-c40278d75b29" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52251806-d785-5838-a46d-8bf20af66d4a" :answer/id #uuid "52251806-d785-5838-a46d-8bf20af66d4a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b60038db-477f-5ef7-9af4-1e5925f39ccc" :answer/id #uuid "b60038db-477f-5ef7-9af4-1e5925f39ccc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09fca4ca-1aba-590b-8f95-45dedb57d0bd" :answer/id #uuid "09fca4ca-1aba-590b-8f95-45dedb57d0bd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186069984 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$  First, we create a table of values.\n\n| $x$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $f(x)$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nNotice that we included both positive and negative input values since the cube root is defined over all real numbers.\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/06/06fb1e18af5c6069d8d0ca0d4ac2e7c3d8103f7b3e672091d05727323ff0d248.png)\n\nSome key points to note:\n\n- The graph passes through the points $(0,0)$ and $(1,1).$\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- Its range is $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- The function is increasing throughout its entire domain.\n\n- As $x \\rightarrow \\infty,$ we have $y\\rightarrow \\infty,$ though the function increases more slowly as $x$ gets large.\n\n- As $x \\rightarrow -\\infty,$ we have $y\\rightarrow -\\infty,$  though the function decreases more slowly as $x$ becomes very negative.\n\nIt is also worth noting that $f(x) =\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is an invertible function, and its inverse is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = x^3, \\qquad x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nThe graph of $f(x) = \\sqrt[3]{x}$ and its inverse are shown below.\n\n![](images/0c/0cb78384fd1ce682758df76ac9b014b105de080b5923df2b313598b8fc7eae46.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068981 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064343 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c428807e-3362-57cf-8f18-309a6336affe"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c428807e-3362-57cf-8f18-309a6336affe" :question/id #uuid "c428807e-3362-57cf-8f18-309a6336affe" :question/math-academy-id "q-10685" :question/problem "What is the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=3{x}^{4}+12{x}^{2}+2\\sqrt{x}$ at the point $(1,17)?$" :question/worked-solution "This time, we'll use $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ notation. First, we compute the derivative, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{4}+12{x}^{2}+2{x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3{x}^{3}+2\\cdot 12x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2{x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =12{x}^{3}+24x+{x}^{-1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & =12(1)^{3}+24(1)+(1)^{-1/2} \\\\  & =12+24+1 \\\\  & =37.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve is $37.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f1af0502-f90d-51c8-983f-2a023e69d3fb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f1af0502-f90d-51c8-983f-2a023e69d3fb" :answer-field/id #uuid "f1af0502-f90d-51c8-983f-2a023e69d3fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-96e8e335-61a7-558f-af2f-626fcdb03817" :answer-field/choices ["answer-96e8e335-61a7-558f-af2f-626fcdb03817" "answer-c8c4ee29-a991-5ae6-951a-7337721cee54" "answer-63e3b5b0-8900-5d3b-a978-40215b9d1186" "answer-81aaba10-9584-56fc-820a-b7e4eac20857" "answer-f3dd1db2-27d9-5d30-b720-d5b687f99de0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96e8e335-61a7-558f-af2f-626fcdb03817" :answer/id #uuid "96e8e335-61a7-558f-af2f-626fcdb03817" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "37" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c8c4ee29-a991-5ae6-951a-7337721cee54" :answer/id #uuid "c8c4ee29-a991-5ae6-951a-7337721cee54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63e3b5b0-8900-5d3b-a978-40215b9d1186" :answer/id #uuid "63e3b5b0-8900-5d3b-a978-40215b9d1186" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "45" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81aaba10-9584-56fc-820a-b7e4eac20857" :answer/id #uuid "81aaba10-9584-56fc-820a-b7e4eac20857" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{37}^{1/2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3dd1db2-27d9-5d30-b720-d5b687f99de0" :answer/id #uuid "f3dd1db2-27d9-5d30-b720-d5b687f99de0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{45}^{1/4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064259 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bc8ddc72-3ceb-5b31-bf69-44ea85d72dfb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064342 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-def70879-cf84-5b54-bdb7-d96481c22cc6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-def70879-cf84-5b54-bdb7-d96481c22cc6" :question/id #uuid "def70879-cf84-5b54-bdb7-d96481c22cc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-342102" :question/problem "Find the exact value of the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=2{x}^{5}-8{x}^{4}+24{x}^{2}-16$ at the point where $x=3.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{5}-8{x}^{4}+24{x}^{2}-16.$ The slope of the tangent at $x=3$ is equal to ${f}^{′}(3).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{5}-8{x}^{4}+24{x}^{2}-16) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 5{x}^{4}-8\\cdot 4{x}^{3}+24\\cdot 2x-0 \\\\  & =10{x}^{4}-32{x}^{3}+48x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & =10({3}^{4})-32({3}^{3})+48(3) \\\\  & =810-864+144 \\\\  & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=3$ is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(3)=90.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-51c33fc1-b49f-5319-ad00-3e78fb97a0a3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-51c33fc1-b49f-5319-ad00-3e78fb97a0a3" :answer-field/id #uuid "51c33fc1-b49f-5319-ad00-3e78fb97a0a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-47783d62-c870-5147-b953-7ccd97997d07" :answer-field/choices ["answer-47783d62-c870-5147-b953-7ccd97997d07"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47783d62-c870-5147-b953-7ccd97997d07" :answer/id #uuid "47783d62-c870-5147-b953-7ccd97997d07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "90" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bc8ddc72-3ceb-5b31-bf69-44ea85d72dfb" :question/id #uuid "bc8ddc72-3ceb-5b31-bf69-44ea85d72dfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-342248" :question/problem "Find the exact value of the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+12x$ whose tangent has a slope of $0.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+12x.$ The slope of the tangent at the point $x$ is equal to ${f}^{′}(x).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+12x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-12x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-12x+12 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent is equal to $0$ at the point where $x=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-40385704-7f81-5553-874b-03df9d2fbb08"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-40385704-7f81-5553-874b-03df9d2fbb08" :answer-field/id #uuid "40385704-7f81-5553-874b-03df9d2fbb08" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c8bcc3fc-12cf-5236-886b-39227298c64c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c8bcc3fc-12cf-5236-886b-39227298c64c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c8bcc3fc-12cf-5236-886b-39227298c64c" :answer/id #uuid "c8bcc3fc-12cf-5236-886b-39227298c64c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-46c1e53f-62e6-5392-ab4c-e6bdacff7e8d" :activity/id #uuid "46c1e53f-62e6-5392-ab4c-e6bdacff7e8d" :activity/title "Calculating the Slope of a Tangent Line Using Differentiation" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050711 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-59f40366-9408-58ed-ba6e-660cf3a0f426" "step-7aefa325-8ae5-5542-9f23-5d3b6f99204e" "step-db280b25-45ac-528e-b41a-e111ae18844b" "step-106e19b3-24d9-5a35-a92b-54bd6690b59f"] :activity/first-step "step-59f40366-9408-58ed-ba6e-660cf3a0f426" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-59f40366-9408-58ed-ba6e-660cf3a0f426" :step/id #uuid "59f40366-9408-58ed-ba6e-660cf3a0f426" :step/content "question-c428807e-3362-57cf-8f18-309a6336affe" :step/next "step-7aefa325-8ae5-5542-9f23-5d3b6f99204e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7aefa325-8ae5-5542-9f23-5d3b6f99204e" :step/id #uuid "7aefa325-8ae5-5542-9f23-5d3b6f99204e" :step/content 17592186329926 :step/next "step-db280b25-45ac-528e-b41a-e111ae18844b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-db280b25-45ac-528e-b41a-e111ae18844b" :step/id #uuid "db280b25-45ac-528e-b41a-e111ae18844b" :step/content "question-def70879-cf84-5b54-bdb7-d96481c22cc6" :step/next "step-106e19b3-24d9-5a35-a92b-54bd6690b59f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-106e19b3-24d9-5a35-a92b-54bd6690b59f" :step/id #uuid "106e19b3-24d9-5a35-a92b-54bd6690b59f" :step/content "question-bc8ddc72-3ceb-5b31-bf69-44ea85d72dfb" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115369 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0fb80f93-0987-58b5-ba12-42ac451dfbe6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-0fb80f93-0987-58b5-ba12-42ac451dfbe6" :question/id #uuid "0fb80f93-0987-58b5-ba12-42ac451dfbe6" :question/math-academy-id "q-317323" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+6?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/01/0183b9256896263aadfb0d18ae0a88aa11152c6e7e7d5693771f2e5bac20ac0a.png)\n\n \n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/31/31c5930bbeca76437d8e7a642bf52da4814bc5afda681b7974b383d3b8762c21.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $6$ units up to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+6.$ The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(0,5)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=6.$\n\n![](images/c4/c4092a81a26d34360480e2ee46e99a17fa740b398038f36de427f3ed839bc68b.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e9033da7-e2fe-5546-be78-e76b67621ec0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e9033da7-e2fe-5546-be78-e76b67621ec0" :answer-field/id #uuid "e9033da7-e2fe-5546-be78-e76b67621ec0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ef42d8b8-af1b-506e-8041-6901cf5ee8e9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dac4b509-3d83-5182-be7b-2caa46451772" "answer-ef42d8b8-af1b-506e-8041-6901cf5ee8e9" "answer-0153aeb0-58c4-5c73-8036-9df357871177" "answer-94c833a6-4aa0-58ca-9b4d-89b409187095" "answer-7c125b62-5ac5-55f9-9f39-b1d75b192a8f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dac4b509-3d83-5182-be7b-2caa46451772" :answer/id #uuid "dac4b509-3d83-5182-be7b-2caa46451772" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/3f/3f357f157e2274b4c15a336c8bf2f15a38d44c8e633cc74a4534931666e910cf.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef42d8b8-af1b-506e-8041-6901cf5ee8e9" :answer/id #uuid "ef42d8b8-af1b-506e-8041-6901cf5ee8e9" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/6a/6ae77b6ce2d7f8c7541ee09c997dcbad86bc4aa40ff330a42c9c755271405796.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0153aeb0-58c4-5c73-8036-9df357871177" :answer/id #uuid "0153aeb0-58c4-5c73-8036-9df357871177" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/73/73ab06d0194c397473319fcda2067761b8ac9b8d34333ffa1316552c4de8a8d0.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94c833a6-4aa0-58ca-9b4d-89b409187095" :answer/id #uuid "94c833a6-4aa0-58ca-9b4d-89b409187095" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/9d/9d811e234521fda8d08a58dd39c1fe6f3e8dce43a4e197a736c340fafd9649a5.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c125b62-5ac5-55f9-9f39-b1d75b192a8f" :answer/id #uuid "7c125b62-5ac5-55f9-9f39-b1d75b192a8f" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/b9/b912b5f205a6baf20a0e86d7bcb521651278db92af6480417ca1e8f2e0197382.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115454 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186187350 :answer-field/choices 17592186187351] [:db/retract 17592186187350 :answer-field/choices 17592186187352] [:db/retract 17592186187350 :answer-field/choices 17592186187353] [:db/retract 17592186187350 :answer-field/choices 17592186187354] [:db/retract 17592186187350 :answer-field/choices 17592186187355] {:db/id 17592186188358 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-856af2a4-8ad4-5acc-a980-a089f44a32f5" "question-02209bdf-5c25-5d71-9f0c-90055349806b" "question-86ba6c5d-d640-5e57-88b7-9b3f13232b7b" "question-449c39ae-346f-5e58-9576-63532dc73237" "question-431dc348-674b-5da2-8e50-c9106a497552"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188066 :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for $(x,y) \\in [0,1] \\times [0,2],$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)= \\dfrac{xy}{8}(2x^2+y).\n$$\n\nFind $F_X(x),$ the marginal CDF of $X,$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1.$" :question/worked-solution "The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF_X(x) = F(x,\\infty) = \\lim\\limits_{y \\to \\infty} F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\leq x\\leq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{X}(x) & =F(x,\\infty ) \\\\  & =F(x,2) \\\\  & =\\frac{x(2)}{8}(2{x}^{2}+(2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4x}{8}({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{3}+x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188277 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3a66a770-559e-57c4-8545-74993f2211db" "question-a27194f1-04ae-54c8-8fbb-2f24bc3ed222" "question-e1859419-9341-5ec9-877c-8ef2835f890f" "question-b3af2400-05b8-5370-91fa-551e055c25be"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188212 :question/problem "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n![](images/d9/d978077685f19e7a958f69c84002e2db1d1ad555a5e4c95bf4236557a5c51cb5.svg)\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x \\geq 1$ and $y\\geq 1.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs $F_X(x)$ and $F_Y(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = F_X(x) \\cdot F_Y (y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $x \\geq 1$ and $y \\geq 1,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{x})(1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}}+\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n![](images/6e/6e5640b83f8602ed4381663f220801276880a024061eb26912f7a28a7f1ffbda.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188298 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-433f0ce6-f1e1-52cc-bff5-350654ecc1ba" "question-74fc0efc-8b80-569c-9c95-98f9405e5eca" "question-2603d8b7-b055-59f9-a9de-a5bcf92cb12b" "question-f97149b0-2afa-55ad-bcc3-a7b6ba9e10a4" "question-fcac8514-54e9-5652-a542-ae9e241a741a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188005 :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n![](images/74/747403765cdc8cdcdad6913f9253d75dc947aab12520d009e25fcd9bd24ad3ae.svg)\n\nFind the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\; y \\geq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x, y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\partial^2}{\\partial x \\partial y} F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $y \\geq 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}(1-{e}^{-y}))) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-{e}^{-y})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{e}^{-y}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-y}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2x{e}^{-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y) = 0.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n![](images/ce/cedf8a240fb8e0642642d5f39a4b174219e9b7c0283a5a3a469f431815e9909b.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-856af2a4-8ad4-5acc-a980-a089f44a32f5" :question/id #uuid "856af2a4-8ad4-5acc-a980-a089f44a32f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-159344" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for $(x,y)\\in [0,1]\\times [0,1],$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{x{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}y}{2}.\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{Y}(y),$ the marginal CDF of $Y,$ for $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=F(\\infty ,y)={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =F(\\infty ,y) \\\\  & =F(1,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{(1){y}^{2}+(1)^{2}y}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}+y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d097562d-4f89-51da-8330-c4683f7fa9ad"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d097562d-4f89-51da-8330-c4683f7fa9ad" :answer-field/id #uuid "d097562d-4f89-51da-8330-c4683f7fa9ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-89657f0a-62ba-5d95-a5f8-5203f8074307" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d033fc14-e820-50e7-b101-809826765d90" "answer-d2c11f33-2f49-5bf1-984d-6f4110492807" "answer-89657f0a-62ba-5d95-a5f8-5203f8074307" "answer-9cde0cbd-f7f5-5917-89ab-3ac53ffe44b3" "answer-13f08af6-0ae0-5946-af18-ac098c2a4f3f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d033fc14-e820-50e7-b101-809826765d90" :answer/id #uuid "d033fc14-e820-50e7-b101-809826765d90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{y+1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2c11f33-2f49-5bf1-984d-6f4110492807" :answer/id #uuid "d2c11f33-2f49-5bf1-984d-6f4110492807" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}+1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89657f0a-62ba-5d95-a5f8-5203f8074307" :answer/id #uuid "89657f0a-62ba-5d95-a5f8-5203f8074307" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}+y}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9cde0cbd-f7f5-5917-89ab-3ac53ffe44b3" :answer/id #uuid "9cde0cbd-f7f5-5917-89ab-3ac53ffe44b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13f08af6-0ae0-5946-af18-ac098c2a4f3f" :answer/id #uuid "13f08af6-0ae0-5946-af18-ac098c2a4f3f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-02209bdf-5c25-5d71-9f0c-90055349806b" :question/id #uuid "02209bdf-5c25-5d71-9f0c-90055349806b" :question/math-academy-id "q-159335" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1\\,\\text{or}\\,y<0 \\\\ \\frac{xy-y}{x}, & x\\ge 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ {F}_{X}(x), & x\\ge 1,\\,y>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{Y}(y),$ the marginal CDF of $Y,$ for $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "The marginal CDF of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=F(\\infty ,y)={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{xy-y}{x}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{y(x-1)}{x}) \\\\  & =y{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1-\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =y{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1-0) \\\\  & =y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-021bcd34-e94e-5e91-97f6-6e6c378cff60"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-021bcd34-e94e-5e91-97f6-6e6c378cff60" :answer-field/id #uuid "021bcd34-e94e-5e91-97f6-6e6c378cff60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9fe28192-dd92-5dbb-b0a6-33f806a9c962" :answer-field/choices ["answer-94939d5f-6f1b-5dd9-ac7a-3a24e4324e85" "answer-1319139a-d5dc-5725-94ac-d3880fa8552c" "answer-0ac8b5d3-4035-5dd3-a5dd-ebf6500b19f9" "answer-9fe28192-dd92-5dbb-b0a6-33f806a9c962" "answer-39126990-5fa4-5f41-bc2f-d291064aff94"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94939d5f-6f1b-5dd9-ac7a-3a24e4324e85" :answer/id #uuid "94939d5f-6f1b-5dd9-ac7a-3a24e4324e85" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1319139a-d5dc-5725-94ac-d3880fa8552c" :answer/id #uuid "1319139a-d5dc-5725-94ac-d3880fa8552c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ac8b5d3-4035-5dd3-a5dd-ebf6500b19f9" :answer/id #uuid "0ac8b5d3-4035-5dd3-a5dd-ebf6500b19f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2y}{y+1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9fe28192-dd92-5dbb-b0a6-33f806a9c962" :answer/id #uuid "9fe28192-dd92-5dbb-b0a6-33f806a9c962" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39126990-5fa4-5f41-bc2f-d291064aff94" :answer/id #uuid "39126990-5fa4-5f41-bc2f-d291064aff94" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-86ba6c5d-d640-5e57-88b7-9b3f13232b7b" :question/id #uuid "86ba6c5d-d640-5e57-88b7-9b3f13232b7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-159337" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\,\\text{or}\\,y<0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y}{2\\pi }, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ {F}_{Y}(y), & x>2,\\,y\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{X}(x),$ the marginal CDF of $X,$ for $0\\le x\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=F(x,\\infty )={lim}_{y\\to \\infty }F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le x\\le 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{X}(x) & ={lim}_{y\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y}{2\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\pi }\\cdot {lim}_{y\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-661c9719-3dbb-5353-ab1d-1d0798143c7b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-661c9719-3dbb-5353-ab1d-1d0798143c7b" :answer-field/id #uuid "661c9719-3dbb-5353-ab1d-1d0798143c7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a54b7301-a104-5d35-8c4d-e29e0811b8dc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a12ca628-2589-5dfb-9c58-44d876d2d743" "answer-3c40ec30-1fb8-58f2-8c9a-e6bd665ac483" "answer-a54b7301-a104-5d35-8c4d-e29e0811b8dc" "answer-1544ee01-f534-5f91-a989-82f6c4f8f30f" "answer-3ade37f7-6413-5d0c-aff6-f6ec6bccd435"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a12ca628-2589-5dfb-9c58-44d876d2d743" :answer/id #uuid "a12ca628-2589-5dfb-9c58-44d876d2d743" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{x}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c40ec30-1fb8-58f2-8c9a-e6bd665ac483" :answer/id #uuid "3c40ec30-1fb8-58f2-8c9a-e6bd665ac483" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a54b7301-a104-5d35-8c4d-e29e0811b8dc" :answer/id #uuid "a54b7301-a104-5d35-8c4d-e29e0811b8dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1544ee01-f534-5f91-a989-82f6c4f8f30f" :answer/id #uuid "1544ee01-f534-5f91-a989-82f6c4f8f30f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ade37f7-6413-5d0c-aff6-f6ec6bccd435" :answer/id #uuid "3ade37f7-6413-5d0c-aff6-f6ec6bccd435" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-449c39ae-346f-5e58-9576-63532dc73237" :question/id #uuid "449c39ae-346f-5e58-9576-63532dc73237" :question/math-academy-id "q-159347" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for $(x,y)\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\times [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)).\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{Y}(y),$ the marginal CDF of $Y,$ for $0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=F(\\infty ,y)={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =F(\\infty ,y) \\\\  & =F(\\frac{\\pi }{2},y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-39cb31e5-27ee-572d-af4c-9fd41cce71e4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-39cb31e5-27ee-572d-af4c-9fd41cce71e4" :answer-field/id #uuid "39cb31e5-27ee-572d-af4c-9fd41cce71e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3f5f67cd-f071-54d3-9386-f77655d8f0ca" :answer-field/choices ["answer-189c31de-c0e9-57cf-bbac-10e821864de1" "answer-3f5f67cd-f071-54d3-9386-f77655d8f0ca" "answer-de7e8486-049c-55a6-a95e-c6f6f07266c4" "answer-f23d80ed-0301-53e6-88a5-ad22e0c8f2a0" "answer-72442ffc-e56c-5203-94fd-73282d5c88c7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-189c31de-c0e9-57cf-bbac-10e821864de1" :answer/id #uuid "189c31de-c0e9-57cf-bbac-10e821864de1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f5f67cd-f071-54d3-9386-f77655d8f0ca" :answer/id #uuid "3f5f67cd-f071-54d3-9386-f77655d8f0ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de7e8486-049c-55a6-a95e-c6f6f07266c4" :answer/id #uuid "de7e8486-049c-55a6-a95e-c6f6f07266c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f23d80ed-0301-53e6-88a5-ad22e0c8f2a0" :answer/id #uuid "f23d80ed-0301-53e6-88a5-ad22e0c8f2a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72442ffc-e56c-5203-94fd-73282d5c88c7" :answer/id #uuid "72442ffc-e56c-5203-94fd-73282d5c88c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-431dc348-674b-5da2-8e50-c9106a497552" :question/id #uuid "431dc348-674b-5da2-8e50-c9106a497552" :question/math-academy-id "q-159349" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for $(x,y)\\in [0,1]\\times [0,1],$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={log}_{2}⁡(1+x{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{Y}(y),$ the marginal CDF of $Y,$ for $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=F(\\infty ,y)={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =F(\\infty ,y) \\\\  & =F(1,y) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(1+(1){y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(1+{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-118ed423-ef91-5a50-ac0b-83fc741088e3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-118ed423-ef91-5a50-ac0b-83fc741088e3" :answer-field/id #uuid "118ed423-ef91-5a50-ac0b-83fc741088e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0048fc20-bfd7-5647-9ed3-5e83e80f7855" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4760e0af-7c17-59e4-9428-b7e2b3770e82" "answer-dd831f4c-c232-5259-91e3-6a8e05498594" "answer-a6923305-e2d1-5771-8647-2ea06b7c3f48" "answer-0048fc20-bfd7-5647-9ed3-5e83e80f7855" "answer-7e75a625-c85d-5866-9506-f4004e7988a1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4760e0af-7c17-59e4-9428-b7e2b3770e82" :answer/id #uuid "4760e0af-7c17-59e4-9428-b7e2b3770e82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-{log}_{2}⁡(2-{y}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd831f4c-c232-5259-91e3-6a8e05498594" :answer/id #uuid "dd831f4c-c232-5259-91e3-6a8e05498594" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-{log}_{2}⁡(2-{y}^{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6923305-e2d1-5771-8647-2ea06b7c3f48" :answer/id #uuid "a6923305-e2d1-5771-8647-2ea06b7c3f48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(1+y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0048fc20-bfd7-5647-9ed3-5e83e80f7855" :answer/id #uuid "0048fc20-bfd7-5647-9ed3-5e83e80f7855" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(1+{y}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e75a625-c85d-5866-9506-f4004e7988a1" :answer/id #uuid "7e75a625-c85d-5866-9506-f4004e7988a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(1+{y}^{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3a66a770-559e-57c4-8545-74993f2211db" :question/id #uuid "3a66a770-559e-57c4-8545-74993f2211db" :question/math-academy-id "q-159320" :question/problem "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{1-y}, & y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}})(1-{e}^{1-y}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}-{e}^{1-y}+\\frac{{e}^{1-y}}{\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}-{e}^{1-y}+\\frac{{e}^{1-y}}{\\sqrt{x}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4808f553-7313-53e6-9c9a-a373717e5764"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4808f553-7313-53e6-9c9a-a373717e5764" :answer-field/id #uuid "4808f553-7313-53e6-9c9a-a373717e5764" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e95e6242-3b0b-565c-aa1a-1ac96cd9dddc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c717cb6b-8e7f-518f-9495-eec458502f12" "answer-88fdae8b-27cd-5d83-8742-cb8138b1a6f3" "answer-e95e6242-3b0b-565c-aa1a-1ac96cd9dddc" "answer-1f14ebf1-a6a5-5a1c-bceb-b94a30140ab2" "answer-e402e686-3ff6-5db1-a2cc-d914d442a9da"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c717cb6b-8e7f-518f-9495-eec458502f12" :answer/id #uuid "c717cb6b-8e7f-518f-9495-eec458502f12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1+\\frac{{e}^{1-y}}{\\sqrt{x}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88fdae8b-27cd-5d83-8742-cb8138b1a6f3" :answer/id #uuid "88fdae8b-27cd-5d83-8742-cb8138b1a6f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}-{e}^{1-y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e95e6242-3b0b-565c-aa1a-1ac96cd9dddc" :answer/id #uuid "e95e6242-3b0b-565c-aa1a-1ac96cd9dddc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}-{e}^{1-y}+\\frac{{e}^{1-y}}{\\sqrt{x}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f14ebf1-a6a5-5a1c-bceb-b94a30140ab2" :answer/id #uuid "1f14ebf1-a6a5-5a1c-bceb-b94a30140ab2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}}-2{e}^{1-y}+\\frac{{e}^{1-y}}{\\sqrt{x}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e402e686-3ff6-5db1-a2cc-d914d442a9da" :answer/id #uuid "e402e686-3ff6-5db1-a2cc-d914d442a9da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\frac{{e}^{1-y}}{\\sqrt{x}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a27194f1-04ae-54c8-8fbb-2f24bc3ed222" :question/id #uuid "a27194f1-04ae-54c8-8fbb-2f24bc3ed222" :question/math-academy-id "q-159322" :question/problem "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2+x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-2y}, & y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(\\frac{x}{2+x})(1-{e}^{-2y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2+x}(1-{e}^{-2y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2+x}(1-{e}^{-2y}), & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-55ed3e7c-fab6-5103-98c8-80e32b0d59cd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-55ed3e7c-fab6-5103-98c8-80e32b0d59cd" :answer-field/id #uuid "55ed3e7c-fab6-5103-98c8-80e32b0d59cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9664a9c8-6635-5edb-95e2-cc6d28ff1c9d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-15aa0740-bb6f-50d4-a937-2834a1c7e53c" "answer-e34a28f2-4fe3-5a59-b680-84b2b53ea3c9" "answer-b69b91e9-4e20-5e6f-b2c3-445a82748c79" "answer-9664a9c8-6635-5edb-95e2-cc6d28ff1c9d" "answer-f54531dc-c152-57ba-b9ee-ccc647ffc034"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15aa0740-bb6f-50d4-a937-2834a1c7e53c" :answer/id #uuid "15aa0740-bb6f-50d4-a937-2834a1c7e53c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\frac{x{e}^{-2y}}{2+x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e34a28f2-4fe3-5a59-b680-84b2b53ea3c9" :answer/id #uuid "e34a28f2-4fe3-5a59-b680-84b2b53ea3c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\frac{x}{2+x}-{e}^{-2y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b69b91e9-4e20-5e6f-b2c3-445a82748c79" :answer/id #uuid "b69b91e9-4e20-5e6f-b2c3-445a82748c79" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{1+x}(1-{e}^{-2y})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9664a9c8-6635-5edb-95e2-cc6d28ff1c9d" :answer/id #uuid "9664a9c8-6635-5edb-95e2-cc6d28ff1c9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2+x}(1-{e}^{-2y})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f54531dc-c152-57ba-b9ee-ccc647ffc034" :answer/id #uuid "f54531dc-c152-57ba-b9ee-ccc647ffc034" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-2y}}{2+x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e1859419-9341-5ec9-877c-8ef2835f890f" :question/id #uuid "e1859419-9341-5ec9-877c-8ef2835f890f" :question/math-academy-id "q-159323" :question/problem "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}, & x\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x\\ge 2$ and $y\\ge 2.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $x\\ge 2$ and $y\\ge 2,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}})(1-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}+\\frac{128}{{x}^{4}{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}+\\frac{128}{{x}^{4}{y}^{3}}, & x\\ge 2,\\,y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-35729131-6ecd-51d1-af5b-b92e2a8eb812"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-35729131-6ecd-51d1-af5b-b92e2a8eb812" :answer-field/id #uuid "35729131-6ecd-51d1-af5b-b92e2a8eb812" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b225b894-490f-581e-bce4-4a2a39058d58" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e29f4e96-d27a-5c57-bc4c-2abd5d1dceda" "answer-01ac938f-f798-5038-bab5-fa297deba0a7" "answer-b225b894-490f-581e-bce4-4a2a39058d58" "answer-254d8e8c-0380-5000-b8d0-a054397414b0" "answer-03d36e20-40e6-5b80-9c0d-67f894b58f3e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e29f4e96-d27a-5c57-bc4c-2abd5d1dceda" :answer/id #uuid "e29f4e96-d27a-5c57-bc4c-2abd5d1dceda" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\frac{128}{{x}^{4}{y}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-01ac938f-f798-5038-bab5-fa297deba0a7" :answer/id #uuid "01ac938f-f798-5038-bab5-fa297deba0a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b225b894-490f-581e-bce4-4a2a39058d58" :answer/id #uuid "b225b894-490f-581e-bce4-4a2a39058d58" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}+\\frac{128}{{x}^{4}{y}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-254d8e8c-0380-5000-b8d0-a054397414b0" :answer/id #uuid "254d8e8c-0380-5000-b8d0-a054397414b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}+\\frac{24}{{x}^{4}{y}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03d36e20-40e6-5b80-9c0d-67f894b58f3e" :answer/id #uuid "03d36e20-40e6-5b80-9c0d-67f894b58f3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\frac{24}{{x}^{4}{y}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b3af2400-05b8-5370-91fa-551e055c25be" :question/id #uuid "b3af2400-05b8-5370-91fa-551e055c25be" :question/math-academy-id "q-159318" :question/problem "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 1, & x>2\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & y<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3}, & 0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 1, & y>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 2,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\text{ or }y<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,y>2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3}, & x>2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 1, & x>2,\\,y>2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b6d562ef-bf77-5294-b08b-2567310b5dfd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b6d562ef-bf77-5294-b08b-2567310b5dfd" :answer-field/id #uuid "b6d562ef-bf77-5294-b08b-2567310b5dfd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4dc14b6f-032f-51fe-a8fa-be5878a1d00c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bcb7fa80-1aa3-58ec-a7a7-78c251d5440b" "answer-0f8acf17-693a-53fb-9034-d0608ef3c3b1" "answer-0a3f1677-b272-57cb-bfa3-3f5d3ab81a1d" "answer-4dc14b6f-032f-51fe-a8fa-be5878a1d00c" "answer-68a4acc5-d960-59ae-98ec-17c1c59437b7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bcb7fa80-1aa3-58ec-a7a7-78c251d5440b" :answer/id #uuid "bcb7fa80-1aa3-58ec-a7a7-78c251d5440b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f8acf17-693a-53fb-9034-d0608ef3c3b1" :answer/id #uuid "0f8acf17-693a-53fb-9034-d0608ef3c3b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a3f1677-b272-57cb-bfa3-3f5d3ab81a1d" :answer/id #uuid "0a3f1677-b272-57cb-bfa3-3f5d3ab81a1d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4dc14b6f-032f-51fe-a8fa-be5878a1d00c" :answer/id #uuid "4dc14b6f-032f-51fe-a8fa-be5878a1d00c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68a4acc5-d960-59ae-98ec-17c1c59437b7" :answer/id #uuid "68a4acc5-d960-59ae-98ec-17c1c59437b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}(2{x}^{2}+{y}^{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188845 :question/problem "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{y}, & y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})(1-\\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186187350 :answer-field/correct "answer-a0b757df-503d-509d-9bb1-a13dcd28aa28" :answer-field/choices ["answer-818e2fbb-826c-599b-9c21-644aa5a7adec" "answer-eee91643-fab4-507a-be86-e3d0341b84c2" "answer-ba331054-dad2-50ac-98dd-c8c0997ac923" "answer-a0b757df-503d-509d-9bb1-a13dcd28aa28" "answer-5ded94d4-f6db-5cd8-9fbd-892a2aec9b10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-818e2fbb-826c-599b-9c21-644aa5a7adec" :answer/id #uuid "818e2fbb-826c-599b-9c21-644aa5a7adec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eee91643-fab4-507a-be86-e3d0341b84c2" :answer/id #uuid "eee91643-fab4-507a-be86-e3d0341b84c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba331054-dad2-50ac-98dd-c8c0997ac923" :answer/id #uuid "ba331054-dad2-50ac-98dd-c8c0997ac923" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0b757df-503d-509d-9bb1-a13dcd28aa28" :answer/id #uuid "a0b757df-503d-509d-9bb1-a13dcd28aa28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ded94d4-f6db-5cd8-9fbd-892a2aec9b10" :answer/id #uuid "5ded94d4-f6db-5cd8-9fbd-892a2aec9b10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-433f0ce6-f1e1-52cc-bff5-350654ecc1ba" :question/id #uuid "433f0ce6-f1e1-52cc-bff5-350654ecc1ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-159327" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\,\\text{or}\\,y<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{48}xy(x+y), & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4 \\\\ {F}_{X}(x), & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,y>4 \\\\ {F}_{Y}(y), & x>2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>2,\\,y>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${F}_{X}$ and ${F}_{Y}$ are the marginal CDFs of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively. Find the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is determined by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{48}xy(x+y))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)+x\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2xy) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+2y\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}(2x+2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}(x+y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{24}(x+y), & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e29738f2-3cd0-58f4-93b4-014da2a887b4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e29738f2-3cd0-58f4-93b4-014da2a887b4" :answer-field/id #uuid "e29738f2-3cd0-58f4-93b4-014da2a887b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0ca9b839-cb5a-5b4b-bc58-e09b93f3700e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3fcd8e4f-e442-55ec-acdd-e2642ab81430" "answer-18bd9257-411d-5063-94ed-ecd8343134ce" "answer-cdab7955-73b1-53f6-a3ff-c803c784a336" "answer-0ca9b839-cb5a-5b4b-bc58-e09b93f3700e" "answer-a0127b97-00fd-5391-a92c-e6076c707eb6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3fcd8e4f-e442-55ec-acdd-e2642ab81430" :answer/id #uuid "3fcd8e4f-e442-55ec-acdd-e2642ab81430" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}(x+y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18bd9257-411d-5063-94ed-ecd8343134ce" :answer/id #uuid "18bd9257-411d-5063-94ed-ecd8343134ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cdab7955-73b1-53f6-a3ff-c803c784a336" :answer/id #uuid "cdab7955-73b1-53f6-a3ff-c803c784a336" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}x(x+y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ca9b839-cb5a-5b4b-bc58-e09b93f3700e" :answer/id #uuid "0ca9b839-cb5a-5b4b-bc58-e09b93f3700e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}(x+y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0127b97-00fd-5391-a92c-e6076c707eb6" :answer/id #uuid "a0127b97-00fd-5391-a92c-e6076c707eb6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-74fc0efc-8b80-569c-9c95-98f9405e5eca" :question/id #uuid "74fc0efc-8b80-569c-9c95-98f9405e5eca" :question/math-academy-id "q-159311" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)({y}^{2}-1)}{x{y}^{2}}, & x\\ge 2,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $x\\ge 2,\\,y\\ge 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ge 2$ and $y\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{(x-2)({y}^{2}-1)}{x{y}^{2}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-2}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-2}{x}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{2}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}, & x\\ge 2,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-20ab855f-f5a7-5070-a185-6c452a9551a0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-20ab855f-f5a7-5070-a185-6c452a9551a0" :answer-field/id #uuid "20ab855f-f5a7-5070-a185-6c452a9551a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9ffd77dc-caa5-5a7b-9077-2effd6a99afc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1f7b246b-a91d-5397-9b3e-492593bcf3f5" "answer-c936279a-3486-5ccb-b9e0-fffe1ccea34f" "answer-f30d0020-1a54-5639-ab8d-73a8529f890a" "answer-096fe8eb-0f73-5dcd-9283-5609e6cf6c6b" "answer-9ffd77dc-caa5-5a7b-9077-2effd6a99afc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f7b246b-a91d-5397-9b3e-492593bcf3f5" :answer/id #uuid "1f7b246b-a91d-5397-9b3e-492593bcf3f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c936279a-3486-5ccb-b9e0-fffe1ccea34f" :answer/id #uuid "c936279a-3486-5ccb-b9e0-fffe1ccea34f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x{y}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f30d0020-1a54-5639-ab8d-73a8529f890a" :answer/id #uuid "f30d0020-1a54-5639-ab8d-73a8529f890a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}{y}^{4}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-096fe8eb-0f73-5dcd-9283-5609e6cf6c6b" :answer/id #uuid "096fe8eb-0f73-5dcd-9283-5609e6cf6c6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{x{y}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ffd77dc-caa5-5a7b-9077-2effd6a99afc" :answer/id #uuid "9ffd77dc-caa5-5a7b-9077-2effd6a99afc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2603d8b7-b055-59f9-a9de-a5bcf92cb12b" :question/id #uuid "2603d8b7-b055-59f9-a9de-a5bcf92cb12b" :question/math-academy-id "q-159330" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\,\\text{or}\\,y<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}{y}^{3/2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ {F}_{X}(x), & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,y>1 \\\\ {F}_{Y}(y), & x>2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 1, & x>2,\\,y>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${F}_{X}$ and ${F}_{Y}$ are the marginal CDFs of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively. Find the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is determined by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}{y}^{3/2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3/2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{1/2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{1/2}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}x{y}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}x{y}^{1/2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-135ebc58-1d65-54f4-8dbd-b35d2b714a66"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-135ebc58-1d65-54f4-8dbd-b35d2b714a66" :answer-field/id #uuid "135ebc58-1d65-54f4-8dbd-b35d2b714a66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-717d95b7-fa98-5ce7-87da-3b5f14244fb8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a5562678-6941-5fda-a0d2-04f3395f2ed7" "answer-da74a110-efe7-5877-a2c6-a01a07f76757" "answer-85f0574f-2eb3-5a2e-a9b4-0e626bc0fe9d" "answer-717d95b7-fa98-5ce7-87da-3b5f14244fb8" "answer-8ad206e5-9b08-5529-8514-60481a8f3392"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5562678-6941-5fda-a0d2-04f3395f2ed7" :answer/id #uuid "a5562678-6941-5fda-a0d2-04f3395f2ed7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{3}{y}^{3/2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da74a110-efe7-5877-a2c6-a01a07f76757" :answer/id #uuid "da74a110-efe7-5877-a2c6-a01a07f76757" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{3/2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85f0574f-2eb3-5a2e-a9b4-0e626bc0fe9d" :answer/id #uuid "85f0574f-2eb3-5a2e-a9b4-0e626bc0fe9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}{y}^{1/2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-717d95b7-fa98-5ce7-87da-3b5f14244fb8" :answer/id #uuid "717d95b7-fa98-5ce7-87da-3b5f14244fb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}x{y}^{1/2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ad206e5-9b08-5529-8514-60481a8f3392" :answer/id #uuid "8ad206e5-9b08-5529-8514-60481a8f3392" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}{y}^{5/2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f97149b0-2afa-55ad-bcc3-a7b6ba9e10a4" :question/id #uuid "f97149b0-2afa-55ad-bcc3-a7b6ba9e10a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-159331" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\,\\text{or}\\,y<1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}({y}^{2}-1)}{3{y}^{2}}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ {F}_{X}(x), & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,y>2 \\\\ {F}_{Y}(y), & x>1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 1, & x>1,\\,y>2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${F}_{X}$ and ${F}_{Y}$ are the marginal CDFs of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively. Find the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $1\\le y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is determined by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $1\\le y\\le 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{4{x}^{2}({y}^{2}-1)}{3{y}^{2}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3{y}^{3}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{16x}{3{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{16x}{3{y}^{3}}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2bb56fd2-9bf7-57d4-98c0-5fd980b76e4e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2bb56fd2-9bf7-57d4-98c0-5fd980b76e4e" :answer-field/id #uuid "2bb56fd2-9bf7-57d4-98c0-5fd980b76e4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fa973044-c950-5daa-a2a0-d7518f5a10c8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fa973044-c950-5daa-a2a0-d7518f5a10c8" "answer-dd56e164-faac-55a7-ae60-d5adf3110db3" "answer-fe148dd0-c4b8-52c9-9a1c-2693969bc24e" "answer-ac47ec72-2d41-5b5a-b068-bbdef9147797" "answer-575f89b2-b860-5bf6-94c2-b2386d5174b1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa973044-c950-5daa-a2a0-d7518f5a10c8" :answer/id #uuid "fa973044-c950-5daa-a2a0-d7518f5a10c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16x}{3{y}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd56e164-faac-55a7-ae60-d5adf3110db3" :answer/id #uuid "dd56e164-faac-55a7-ae60-d5adf3110db3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16{x}^{3}}{9{y}^{4}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe148dd0-c4b8-52c9-9a1c-2693969bc24e" :answer/id #uuid "fe148dd0-c4b8-52c9-9a1c-2693969bc24e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4x}{9{y}^{4}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac47ec72-2d41-5b5a-b068-bbdef9147797" :answer/id #uuid "ac47ec72-2d41-5b5a-b068-bbdef9147797" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{3{y}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-575f89b2-b860-5bf6-94c2-b2386d5174b1" :answer/id #uuid "575f89b2-b860-5bf6-94c2-b2386d5174b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{3{y}^{4}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fcac8514-54e9-5652-a542-ae9e241a741a" :question/id #uuid "fcac8514-54e9-5652-a542-ae9e241a741a" :question/math-academy-id "q-159315" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)(y-1)}{xy}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{(x-1)(y-1)}{xy})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{y})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e0c66bcf-f118-5a15-8c75-354e4b9c987f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e0c66bcf-f118-5a15-8c75-354e4b9c987f" :answer-field/id #uuid "e0c66bcf-f118-5a15-8c75-354e4b9c987f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b08f83bf-773c-562d-b48f-e6657ce17368" :answer-field/choices ["answer-71dd7185-fad4-53e7-b17c-bea3ed88b7f0" "answer-b08f83bf-773c-562d-b48f-e6657ce17368" "answer-d77ff831-4178-5cb0-8b9c-9a40136d4cb4" "answer-4b66b758-0826-5108-9ac0-ade8eefbc63c" "answer-236a2d72-bb0b-50be-bba3-e4d52248a15f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71dd7185-fad4-53e7-b17c-bea3ed88b7f0" :answer/id #uuid "71dd7185-fad4-53e7-b17c-bea3ed88b7f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{xy}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b08f83bf-773c-562d-b48f-e6657ce17368" :answer/id #uuid "b08f83bf-773c-562d-b48f-e6657ce17368" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d77ff831-4178-5cb0-8b9c-9a40136d4cb4" :answer/id #uuid "d77ff831-4178-5cb0-8b9c-9a40136d4cb4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}{y}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b66b758-0826-5108-9ac0-ade8eefbc63c" :answer/id #uuid "4b66b758-0826-5108-9ac0-ade8eefbc63c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{xy}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-236a2d72-bb0b-50be-bba3-e4d52248a15f" :answer/id #uuid "236a2d72-bb0b-50be-bba3-e4d52248a15f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186189432 :tutorial/content "Recall that the **joint cumulative distribution function** (or joint CDF) of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X \\leq x,Y \\leq y).\n$$\n\nIf $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x, y)$, we have the following relationship between the joint PDF and joint CDF:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\int^y_{-\\infty}\\int^x_{-\\infty} f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u\\textrm{d}v.\n$$\n\nGeometrically, the joint CDF gives the probability that the random pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region $\\Delta$ that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\n![](images/ee/ee82c6b810f02f8b8380bd97c94cbe491095d17dbadc685ffdca1a54cf9828b8.png)\n\nThe joint CDF satisfies the following properties:\n\n- $0\\leq F(x,y)\\leq 1$\n\n- $F(\\infty, \\infty)=1$\n\n- $F(-\\infty ,y)=F(x,-\\infty)=0$\n\n- The **marginal CDF** of $X$ is given by $F_X(x)=F(x,\\infty).$\n\n- The **marginal CDF** of $Y$ is given by $F_Y(y)=F(\\infty,y).$\n\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables, then \n$F(x,y)=F_X(x)\\cdot F_Y(y).$\n\n- The joint probability density function $f(x, y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\partial^2}{\\partial x \\partial y} F(x,y).\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we'll explore the last four of these properties in more detail." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186189381 :tutorial/content "The first property to consider is how to recover the marginal CDFs of $X$ and $Y$ from the joint CDF. \n\nSuppose the joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n![](images/ec/ecc23b4f4772364ee423406c3bb030b51129972d7c3c49cb03e35f8aca638485.svg)\n\nLet's calculate $F_Y(y),$ the marginal CDF of $Y$ using the following property:\n\n$$\nF_Y(y) = F(\\infty,y) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} F(x,y)\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $y \\geq 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[(1-{e}^{-x})(1-{e}^{-2y})] \\\\  & =(1-{e}^{-2y}){lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}) \\\\  & =(1-{e}^{-2y}){lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1-0) \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-2y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for the marginal CDF is as follows:\n\n![](images/ae/ae315a3011daa44612e13dec66cc848b5b06ba4002a4baf183e5edabc13ad974.svg)\n\nLet's see another example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188460 :activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105919 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0a8909ee-fe18-5d80-9c79-9f98ec446962" "question-0bf79e97-49c2-5006-b045-7fdc65e739fd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105475 :question/problem "Find the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence given that the first term is $-1$ and the eighth term is $13.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term is given by \n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d.\n$$\n\nWe already know that the first term is $a_1=-1,$ so we just need to figure out the common difference $d.$\n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms $a_1=-1$ and $a_8=13$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{8}-{a}_{1}}{8-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{13-(-1)}{8-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{7} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is  \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\ta_n&= a_{1}+(n-1)d \\[3pt]\n \t\t& = -1 + (n-1) (2) \\[3pt]\n \t\t& = -1 + 2n - 2 \\[3pt]\n\t\t& = 2n - 3.\n\t}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105920 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e88dff2d-42e3-57bd-b14e-035af9fb2260" "question-1617bc78-d7dd-5411-87bd-2e3b8377e377"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105476 :question/problem "The fifth and sixth terms of an arithmetic sequence are $46$ and $50,$ respectively. Determine the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term is given by \n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term $a_1$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nSince the terms $a_5=46$ and $a_6=50$ are consecutive, we can subtract them to find the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{6}-{a}_{5} \\\\  & =50-46 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, $a_5=46,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since $a_5$ is $4$ jumps after $a_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\na_5 &= a_1 + 4d \\[3pt]\n46 &= a_1 + 4\\cdot 4\\[3pt]\n46 &= a_1 +16 \\[3pt]\n46-16 &= a_1\\[3pt]\n      a_1&= 30.\n\t}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\ta_n&= a_{1}+(n-1)d \\[3pt]\n \t\t& = 30 + (n-1) \\cdot 4 \\[3pt]\n \t\t& =30 + 4n -4 \\[3pt]\n\t\t& = 4n +26.\n\t}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105921 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-280035db-18a9-57e4-8578-03884e2e2b26" "question-ae437285-daa1-5ee1-861c-3f25e99e891f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105477 :question/problem "An arithmetic sequence has the $3$rd term $a_{3}=9$ and the $8$th term is $a_{8}=69.$ Find a formula for the $n$th term of the sequence." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term is given by  \n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out what the first term $a_1$ and the common difference $d$ are.\n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms $a_{8}=69$ and $a_{3}=9$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{8}-{a}_{3}}{8-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{69-9}{8-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{5} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, $a_{3}=9,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since $a_{3}$ is $2$ jumps after $a_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}+2d \\\\ 9 & ={a}_{1}+2\\cdot 12 \\\\ 9 & ={a}_{1}+24 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-15+(n-1)\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =-15+12n-12 \\\\  & =12n-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-0a8909ee-fe18-5d80-9c79-9f98ec446962" :question/id #uuid "0a8909ee-fe18-5d80-9c79-9f98ec446962" :question/math-academy-id "q-67258" :question/problem "Find the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the $1$st term is $0$ and the $9$th term is $40.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term is given by \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nWe already know that the first term is ${a}_{1}=0,$ so we just need to figure out the common difference $d.$ \n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms ${a}_{1}=0$ and ${a}_{9}=40$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{9}-{a}_{1}}{9-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{40-0}{9-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{8} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =0+(n-1)(5) \\\\  & =5n-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a2b7787d-35b2-532c-8902-7f97ae2f70d4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a2b7787d-35b2-532c-8902-7f97ae2f70d4" :answer-field/id #uuid "a2b7787d-35b2-532c-8902-7f97ae2f70d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-73277f8a-4dc6-5409-8fd7-120803fdac9d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4c984f91-a4b9-5814-b699-528b5d5712e0" "answer-e0390ac1-96dc-5f85-a891-92dd602cb026" "answer-c093cbe9-ccb0-5011-bba5-8f02aef40b07" "answer-89f223b1-2aae-5a28-93a0-2e2fa10cbab3" "answer-73277f8a-4dc6-5409-8fd7-120803fdac9d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c984f91-a4b9-5814-b699-528b5d5712e0" :answer/id #uuid "4c984f91-a4b9-5814-b699-528b5d5712e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=5-5n" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0390ac1-96dc-5f85-a891-92dd602cb026" :answer/id #uuid "e0390ac1-96dc-5f85-a891-92dd602cb026" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=5n+10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c093cbe9-ccb0-5011-bba5-8f02aef40b07" :answer/id #uuid "c093cbe9-ccb0-5011-bba5-8f02aef40b07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=5n+4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89f223b1-2aae-5a28-93a0-2e2fa10cbab3" :answer/id #uuid "89f223b1-2aae-5a28-93a0-2e2fa10cbab3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=5n-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-73277f8a-4dc6-5409-8fd7-120803fdac9d" :answer/id #uuid "73277f8a-4dc6-5409-8fd7-120803fdac9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=5n-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0bf79e97-49c2-5006-b045-7fdc65e739fd" :question/id #uuid "0bf79e97-49c2-5006-b045-7fdc65e739fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-5430" :question/problem "Find the general formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the $7$th term is $-18$ and the first term is $6.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is given by \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nWe already know that the first term is ${a}_{1}=6,$ so we just need to figure out the common difference $d.$ \n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms ${a}_{1}=6$ and ${a}_{7}=-18$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{7}-{a}_{1}}{7-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-18-6}{7-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-24}{6} \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,  the formula for the $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence is  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =6+(n-1)(-4) \\\\  & =6-4n+4 \\\\  & =10-4n.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-513c7915-c92e-5aaa-be0c-6e034ccddc23"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-513c7915-c92e-5aaa-be0c-6e034ccddc23" :answer-field/id #uuid "513c7915-c92e-5aaa-be0c-6e034ccddc23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4baadab2-f74e-5b1a-a566-4516ff320bc8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c40605ec-6e8c-5d86-88f7-e1a707947506" "answer-d61be220-e0b3-5cf5-ba3d-fb0da48220f4" "answer-74b90514-f9b6-53a8-9a0e-c8f08899b1fd" "answer-4baadab2-f74e-5b1a-a566-4516ff320bc8" "answer-15902cea-7437-56a5-9a4e-bef728799b33"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c40605ec-6e8c-5d86-88f7-e1a707947506" :answer/id #uuid "c40605ec-6e8c-5d86-88f7-e1a707947506" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-25+n" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d61be220-e0b3-5cf5-ba3d-fb0da48220f4" :answer/id #uuid "d61be220-e0b3-5cf5-ba3d-fb0da48220f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-32+2n" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74b90514-f9b6-53a8-9a0e-c8f08899b1fd" :answer/id #uuid "74b90514-f9b6-53a8-9a0e-c8f08899b1fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-4-2n" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4baadab2-f74e-5b1a-a566-4516ff320bc8" :answer/id #uuid "4baadab2-f74e-5b1a-a566-4516ff320bc8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=10-4n" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15902cea-7437-56a5-9a4e-bef728799b33" :answer/id #uuid "15902cea-7437-56a5-9a4e-bef728799b33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=3-3n" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e88dff2d-42e3-57bd-b14e-035af9fb2260" :question/id #uuid "e88dff2d-42e3-57bd-b14e-035af9fb2260" :question/math-academy-id "q-67315" :question/problem "The second and third terms of an arithmetic sequence are $45$ and $80,$ respectively. Determine the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term is given by  \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$ \n\nSince the terms ${a}_{2}=45$ and ${a}_{3}=80$ are consecutive, we can subtract them to find the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{3}-{a}_{2} \\\\  & =80-45 \\\\  & =35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{2}=45,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{2}$ is $1$ jump after ${a}_{1},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}+1\\cdot d \\\\ 45 & ={a}_{1}+35 \\\\ 45-35 & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =10+(n-1)\\cdot 35 \\\\  & =10+35n-35 \\\\  & =35n-25\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-43336448-fdb3-5bd6-87e6-58e2f3e34475"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-43336448-fdb3-5bd6-87e6-58e2f3e34475" :answer-field/id #uuid "43336448-fdb3-5bd6-87e6-58e2f3e34475" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-aef36b79-0fca-55a0-82c2-a387aff7f592" :answer-field/choices ["answer-be92e317-fad1-556b-9b81-60c3646e4319" "answer-468640b9-af11-542d-b37d-d31de6796ec8" "answer-aef36b79-0fca-55a0-82c2-a387aff7f592" "answer-9d8ed075-b778-5d73-9752-f895a8ed1f58" "answer-19d8b2ad-600e-5ebd-8a78-d74cb33e71e3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be92e317-fad1-556b-9b81-60c3646e4319" :answer/id #uuid "be92e317-fad1-556b-9b81-60c3646e4319" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=15n+15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-468640b9-af11-542d-b37d-d31de6796ec8" :answer/id #uuid "468640b9-af11-542d-b37d-d31de6796ec8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=20n+5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aef36b79-0fca-55a0-82c2-a387aff7f592" :answer/id #uuid "aef36b79-0fca-55a0-82c2-a387aff7f592" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=35n-25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d8ed075-b778-5d73-9752-f895a8ed1f58" :answer/id #uuid "9d8ed075-b778-5d73-9752-f895a8ed1f58" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=5n+25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19d8b2ad-600e-5ebd-8a78-d74cb33e71e3" :answer/id #uuid "19d8b2ad-600e-5ebd-8a78-d74cb33e71e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=9n+27" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1617bc78-d7dd-5411-87bd-2e3b8377e377" :question/id #uuid "1617bc78-d7dd-5411-87bd-2e3b8377e377" :question/math-academy-id "q-177698" :question/problem "The $7$th and $8$th terms of an arithmetic sequence are $-13$ and $-20,$ respectively. Determine the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term is given by \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$ \n\nSince the terms ${a}_{7}=-13$ and ${a}_{8}=-20$ are consecutive, we can subtract them to find the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{8}-{a}_{7} \\\\  & =-20-(-13) \\\\  & =-20+13 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{8}=-20,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{8}$ is $7$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{8} & ={a}_{1}+7d \\\\ -20 & ={a}_{1}+7(-7) \\\\ -20 & ={a}_{1}-49 \\\\ -20+49 & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =29+(n-1)(-7) \\\\  & =29-7n+7 \\\\  & =36-7n.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-16ee1412-300a-5b7c-8672-5b2fe0bb6427"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-16ee1412-300a-5b7c-8672-5b2fe0bb6427" :answer-field/id #uuid "16ee1412-300a-5b7c-8672-5b2fe0bb6427" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-39dd4436-0462-52a9-8a76-ff3378d47742" :answer-field/choices ["answer-29d8449f-a2a9-5f5d-8194-1fb9fc01dd54" "answer-5cd38354-9ff1-5302-951a-56937f006954" "answer-39dd4436-0462-52a9-8a76-ff3378d47742" "answer-8890407d-9b09-50bd-93bb-97a5aad99850" "answer-f8d5b5fe-9067-555e-9c87-d2f8bc76fbac"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29d8449f-a2a9-5f5d-8194-1fb9fc01dd54" :answer/id #uuid "29d8449f-a2a9-5f5d-8194-1fb9fc01dd54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=29-6n" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5cd38354-9ff1-5302-951a-56937f006954" :answer/id #uuid "5cd38354-9ff1-5302-951a-56937f006954" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=29-7n" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39dd4436-0462-52a9-8a76-ff3378d47742" :answer/id #uuid "39dd4436-0462-52a9-8a76-ff3378d47742" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=36-7n" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8890407d-9b09-50bd-93bb-97a5aad99850" :answer/id #uuid "8890407d-9b09-50bd-93bb-97a5aad99850" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=36-8n" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8d5b5fe-9067-555e-9c87-d2f8bc76fbac" :answer/id #uuid "f8d5b5fe-9067-555e-9c87-d2f8bc76fbac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=43-8n" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-280035db-18a9-57e4-8578-03884e2e2b26" :question/id #uuid "280035db-18a9-57e4-8578-03884e2e2b26" :question/math-academy-id "q-177715" :question/problem "Find the $14$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the $3$rd term is $40$ and the $23$rd term is $-20.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\n \nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms ${a}_{3}=40$ and ${a}_{23}=-20$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{23}-{a}_{3}}{23-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-20-40}{23-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-60)}{20} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{3}=40,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{3}$ is $2$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}+2d \\\\ 40 & ={a}_{1}+2(-3) \\\\ 40 & ={a}_{1}-6 \\\\ 40+6 & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =46.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =46+(n-1)(-3) \\\\  & =46-3n+3 \\\\  & =49-3n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find the $14$th term by substituting $n=14$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{14} & =49-3(14) \\\\  & =49-42 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3f892e58-152a-5784-8094-f9feb6a190be"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3f892e58-152a-5784-8094-f9feb6a190be" :answer-field/id #uuid "3f892e58-152a-5784-8094-f9feb6a190be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-38603c9f-ce2f-5228-bc82-cd69f03c4051" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cd62b352-c3ca-5fbf-8c06-9fd7dbe35fbc" "answer-a74d176b-cd65-53b6-99a3-de18df35d996" "answer-b0cce27d-ad6b-5b49-88ad-8ff0f21d9e3e" "answer-942de8a9-dc38-58f8-b919-a9c435176e8f" "answer-38603c9f-ce2f-5228-bc82-cd69f03c4051"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd62b352-c3ca-5fbf-8c06-9fd7dbe35fbc" :answer/id #uuid "cd62b352-c3ca-5fbf-8c06-9fd7dbe35fbc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a74d176b-cd65-53b6-99a3-de18df35d996" :answer/id #uuid "a74d176b-cd65-53b6-99a3-de18df35d996" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0cce27d-ad6b-5b49-88ad-8ff0f21d9e3e" :answer/id #uuid "b0cce27d-ad6b-5b49-88ad-8ff0f21d9e3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-942de8a9-dc38-58f8-b919-a9c435176e8f" :answer/id #uuid "942de8a9-dc38-58f8-b919-a9c435176e8f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38603c9f-ce2f-5228-bc82-cd69f03c4051" :answer/id #uuid "38603c9f-ce2f-5228-bc82-cd69f03c4051" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ae437285-daa1-5ee1-861c-3f25e99e891f" :question/id #uuid "ae437285-daa1-5ee1-861c-3f25e99e891f" :question/math-academy-id "q-5436" :question/problem "Find the  $11$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the $19$th term is $99$ and the $4$th term is $-6.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term is given by  \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms ${a}_{19}=99$ and ${a}_{4}=-6$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{19}-{a}_{4}}{19-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{99-(-6)}{19-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{105}{15} \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{4}=-6,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{4}$ is $3$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}+3d \\\\ -6 & ={a}_{1}+3\\cdot 7 \\\\ -6 & ={a}_{1}+21 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-27+(n-1)\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =7n-34\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find the $11$th term by substituting $n=11$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & =7(11)-34 \\\\  & =77-34 \\\\  & =43\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-daf5cb02-1913-55ab-ada6-fd6ff2e54598"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-daf5cb02-1913-55ab-ada6-fd6ff2e54598" :answer-field/id #uuid "daf5cb02-1913-55ab-ada6-fd6ff2e54598" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2a6ce4a8-5eae-5199-9d8b-b540d1db3c5c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-db55de2e-167e-50d1-b5d8-8a688cc5cb3a" "answer-4a64c24b-78bb-5b96-a92b-a82e8b8b6cbc" "answer-2a6ce4a8-5eae-5199-9d8b-b540d1db3c5c" "answer-1d2cd7eb-ba98-5414-a771-7bc2d60594d7" "answer-53cdf364-07fb-51ec-b7c3-c207679b3f6a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db55de2e-167e-50d1-b5d8-8a688cc5cb3a" :answer/id #uuid "db55de2e-167e-50d1-b5d8-8a688cc5cb3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "37" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a64c24b-78bb-5b96-a92b-a82e8b8b6cbc" :answer/id #uuid "4a64c24b-78bb-5b96-a92b-a82e8b8b6cbc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "39" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a6ce4a8-5eae-5199-9d8b-b540d1db3c5c" :answer/id #uuid "2a6ce4a8-5eae-5199-9d8b-b540d1db3c5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "43" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d2cd7eb-ba98-5414-a771-7bc2d60594d7" :answer/id #uuid "1d2cd7eb-ba98-5414-a771-7bc2d60594d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "47" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53cdf364-07fb-51ec-b7c3-c207679b3f6a" :answer/id #uuid "53cdf364-07fb-51ec-b7c3-c207679b3f6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "49" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107164 :tutorial/content "To find the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence given two arbitrary terms, we must usually apply the following:\n\n- The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d\n$$\n\nwhere $a_n$ is the $n$th term, $a_1$ is the first term, and $d$ is the common difference.\n\n- We can find the common difference $d$ of an arithmetic sequence given two arbitrary terms using\n\n$$\nd = \\frac{a_m-a_k}{m-k}\n$$\n\nwhere $a_m$ is the $m$th term, and $a_k$ is the $k$th term.\n\nLet's see some examples." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105984 :activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129318 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7a3648d6-4396-5f3b-a4a5-7bdad067d750"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-7a3648d6-4396-5f3b-a4a5-7bdad067d750" :question/id #uuid "7a3648d6-4396-5f3b-a4a5-7bdad067d750" :question/math-academy-id "q-138973" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y={sin}^{2}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\pi .$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{2}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot |2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bc195312-1e76-599e-a6d3-b22fd38f25b6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bc195312-1e76-599e-a6d3-b22fd38f25b6" :answer-field/id #uuid "bc195312-1e76-599e-a6d3-b22fd38f25b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d0da0530-4038-57b2-ad9c-a85207241016" :answer-field/choices ["answer-45595bd3-a3a8-53e3-bf8d-e8a743e2acff" "answer-c04ad9e8-50e3-5c90-8826-dc328c55a80c" "answer-053308a9-7001-5846-9b1c-aebce9551076" "answer-53f5cfc8-bae2-544a-87f1-7473d053b227" "answer-d0da0530-4038-57b2-ad9c-a85207241016"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45595bd3-a3a8-53e3-bf8d-e8a743e2acff" :answer/id #uuid "45595bd3-a3a8-53e3-bf8d-e8a743e2acff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c04ad9e8-50e3-5c90-8826-dc328c55a80c" :answer/id #uuid "c04ad9e8-50e3-5c90-8826-dc328c55a80c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{4}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-053308a9-7001-5846-9b1c-aebce9551076" :answer/id #uuid "053308a9-7001-5846-9b1c-aebce9551076" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+3{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53f5cfc8-bae2-544a-87f1-7473d053b227" :answer/id #uuid "53f5cfc8-bae2-544a-87f1-7473d053b227" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0da0530-4038-57b2-ad9c-a85207241016" :answer/id #uuid "d0da0530-4038-57b2-ad9c-a85207241016" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186396318 :question/problem "Consider a function $b(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{b(t)\\}=B(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{t}b(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{t}b(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,b(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172103 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ac6c0c1f-be4b-5795-8838-93be63fc1a08"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ac6c0c1f-be4b-5795-8838-93be63fc1a08" :question/id #uuid "ac6c0c1f-be4b-5795-8838-93be63fc1a08" :question/math-academy-id "q-139125" :question/problem "The displacement $r,$ in meters, of a particle $P$ of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨{t}^{3},\\,{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the magnitude of the resultant force acting on the particle when $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$ Round your final answer to the nearest Newton." :question/worked-solution "Newton’s second law states that\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the *resultant* force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo compute the acceleration vector, we first differentiate $r$ to get the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2},\\,4{t}^{3}-2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate the velocity vector to get the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6t,\\,12{t}^{2}-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resultant force $F$ at time $t$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =3\\cdot ⟨6t,\\,12{t}^{2}-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨18t,\\,36{t}^{2}-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=4\\,\\text{s},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(4) & =⟨18(4),\\,36({4}^{2})-6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨72,\\,570⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of $F(4)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|F(4)| & =\\sqrt{(72)^{2}+{(570)}^{2}} \\\\  & ≈575\\,\\text{N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest Newton." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-31c651ea-1589-526b-91c7-bd4b6db7a166"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-31c651ea-1589-526b-91c7-bd4b6db7a166" :answer-field/id #uuid "31c651ea-1589-526b-91c7-bd4b6db7a166" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c6aedca6-99db-59f9-95e2-e9fdad4e753f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-87807497-9d16-516b-a885-3af2d03156ea" "answer-beccc26a-d2bc-5b22-9004-128752f9150f" "answer-9653eea3-31da-5fdd-992a-434023dc2787" "answer-c6aedca6-99db-59f9-95e2-e9fdad4e753f" "answer-8965d7e6-0eda-5e94-9abc-17673bfc7c7d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87807497-9d16-516b-a885-3af2d03156ea" :answer/id #uuid "87807497-9d16-516b-a885-3af2d03156ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "430\\,\\text{N}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-beccc26a-d2bc-5b22-9004-128752f9150f" :answer/id #uuid "beccc26a-d2bc-5b22-9004-128752f9150f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "491\\,\\text{N}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9653eea3-31da-5fdd-992a-434023dc2787" :answer/id #uuid "9653eea3-31da-5fdd-992a-434023dc2787" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "511\\,\\text{N}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6aedca6-99db-59f9-95e2-e9fdad4e753f" :answer/id #uuid "c6aedca6-99db-59f9-95e2-e9fdad4e753f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "575\\,\\text{N}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8965d7e6-0eda-5e94-9abc-17673bfc7c7d" :answer/id #uuid "8965d7e6-0eda-5e94-9abc-17673bfc7c7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "612\\,\\text{N}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186411821 :answer-field/correct "answer-d8681e3a-87bd-5430-9dea-17ec9e0977ab" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d8681e3a-87bd-5430-9dea-17ec9e0977ab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8681e3a-87bd-5430-9dea-17ec9e0977ab" :answer/id #uuid "d8681e3a-87bd-5430-9dea-17ec9e0977ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2(t-2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064315 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2f85c49f-b8cd-5032-99b8-bc70560e429d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-2f85c49f-b8cd-5032-99b8-bc70560e429d" :question/id #uuid "2f85c49f-b8cd-5032-99b8-bc70560e429d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143507" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{75}$ be a random sample of size $n=75$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.75,$ calculate $P(1.5<\\overset{―}{X}<1.6).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8643\\,$ | $\\,0.8665\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =75,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }x\\cdot \\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2{b}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =1.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.75.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(1.5,\\frac{0.75}{75}) \\\\  & ∼N(1.5,0.01).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(1.5<\\overset{―}{X}<1.6).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1.5<\\overset{―}{X}<1.6) & =P(\\frac{1.5-1.5}{\\sqrt{0.01}}<Z<\\frac{1.6-1.5}{\\sqrt{0.01}}) \\\\  & =P(0<Z<1) \\\\  & =P(Z<1)-P(Z<0) \\\\  & =Φ(1)-Φ(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1)=0.8413,\n$$\n\nand by the symmetry of the normal distribution, $Φ(0)=0.5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1.5<\\overset{―}{X}<1.6) & =Φ(1)-Φ(0) \\\\  & =0.8413-0.5 \\\\  & =0.3413.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9e93d9e4-a205-5e33-a4db-5e5a5cd44ab1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9e93d9e4-a205-5e33-a4db-5e5a5cd44ab1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9e93d9e4-a205-5e33-a4db-5e5a5cd44ab1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-654e6669-1c8f-5b7c-9105-507f8fec4f7d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0048806c-59ec-5b54-9603-d1aea47812e8" "answer-7e5e4424-4f3f-57f4-b362-4a5859daccd7" "answer-654e6669-1c8f-5b7c-9105-507f8fec4f7d" "answer-b98dc844-9500-5f8d-b857-bb196f769cd6" "answer-e03cd40f-b9b7-5e51-8c05-cf33954b77fd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0048806c-59ec-5b54-9603-d1aea47812e8" :answer/id #uuid "0048806c-59ec-5b54-9603-d1aea47812e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3159" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e5e4424-4f3f-57f4-b362-4a5859daccd7" :answer/id #uuid "7e5e4424-4f3f-57f4-b362-4a5859daccd7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3212" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-654e6669-1c8f-5b7c-9105-507f8fec4f7d" :answer/id #uuid "654e6669-1c8f-5b7c-9105-507f8fec4f7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3413" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b98dc844-9500-5f8d-b857-bb196f769cd6" :answer/id #uuid "b98dc844-9500-5f8d-b857-bb196f769cd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3461" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e03cd40f-b9b7-5e51-8c05-cf33954b77fd" :answer/id #uuid "e03cd40f-b9b7-5e51-8c05-cf33954b77fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3665" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186398726 :question/worked-solution "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/9f/9fb8c20672705e9d68e69d32e7ff3499ec71560e10aa85136b3f3ad75c5fb5ed.png)\n\nNotice that to transform the function $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we reflect $y=u(t)$ in the $y$-axis, which gives $y=u(-t).$\n\n- Next, we shift by two units to the right, giving $y=u(-(t-2))=u(2-t).$\n\n- Finally, we scale by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=2u(2-t).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=2u(2-t).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186118041 :answer-field/choices 17592186118042] [:db/retract 17592186118041 :answer-field/choices 17592186118043] [:db/retract 17592186118041 :answer-field/choices 17592186118044] [:db/retract 17592186118041 :answer-field/choices 17592186118045] [:db/retract 17592186118041 :answer-field/choices 17592186118046] {:db/id 17592186119751 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-519a97bb-018e-5643-989a-45e979fef136"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119567 :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, its vertex at the origin, and it rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/46/46e41d84cb00ea2a95f3d1c94e75863707fddf78ea7117de1ed415b71e66f2f1.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119752 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-093fd520-21d8-5c0d-89e8-fac985077f71"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119568 :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi} 5\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = \n\t72^\\circ.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}$ is equivalent to $72^\\circ.$ \n\nThen, we recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, its vertex at the origin, and it rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/64/6479f08add76d919644c8eba7df6bb1d273fd1de808d4af58876a6a5da539fcb.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119754 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a843ee14-f56b-58b5-bfd0-4708e1493281"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119570 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that:\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, and its vertex is at the origin.\n\n- It rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/1d/1dcf96192164d9e1845207773d4fe9596ff3fd0d5fa7e83984260004e8bc32aa.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119753 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ba6955a5-f1b2-5bb6-bbb0-ee805b7e5b79" "question-d86e19a0-8ae0-5dcf-aad3-3a9ae2b60335" "question-ba6b8db9-c5f9-50b9-8e8a-d50f12658a32"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119569 :question/problem "![](images/f0/f0347081906740a955f8a192baa53c65536b110df9adc6021288c43767c9ac2f.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(\\sqrt 3, 1),$ calculate the angle $\\theta.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/3e/3e5ebde9ee4132e0bd64cb2aaf2a3763abc2b0de1a798e87ed4ee652ca360078.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt 3} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arctan{\\left( \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3} \\right)} = 30^\\circ.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-519a97bb-018e-5643-989a-45e979fef136" :question/id #uuid "519a97bb-018e-5643-989a-45e979fef136" :question/math-academy-id "q-64489" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams shows a unit circle in the coordinate plane with a central angle of ${135}^{∘}$ in the standard position?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis and a vertex that lies on the origin.\n\n- A positive angle has a rotation that is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/e3/e3e61526a1d903bc577d6e2d255f775567ad026a778f20cb8892ee8611c11a02.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-656c251f-6760-57f1-a7e6-0aaf9a089e9c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-656c251f-6760-57f1-a7e6-0aaf9a089e9c" :answer-field/id #uuid "656c251f-6760-57f1-a7e6-0aaf9a089e9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-75be9660-9557-53b4-8002-1434074fd531" :answer-field/choices ["answer-abbd780b-87da-59d0-af9c-5a39def8194c" "answer-082681df-6957-5ca5-8dcb-fd87462adcf0" "answer-984f745f-394c-57ae-bda3-d51a21f36fa7" "answer-75be9660-9557-53b4-8002-1434074fd531" "answer-0d5fe7cf-2df7-57dc-97a8-841f144a4844"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-abbd780b-87da-59d0-af9c-5a39def8194c" :answer/id #uuid "abbd780b-87da-59d0-af9c-5a39def8194c" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/2f/2f6ba2e7e5c4e41d1c41fe4a882a7e14a93631a98e05a24db8a4a11785675f6d.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "ans