 "q-143850" :question/problem "Find $\\text{E}[4(X-1)],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4},\\, & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le {x}^{2}; \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=4(x-1).\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le {x}^{2}\\},$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](images/bd/bd8d2d36a0fe570e6ccdb096c6c39bc9c9edeed9b008dada504801025055438f.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[4(X-1)] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}4(x-1)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4(x-1)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{{x}^{2}}4(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{2}(x-1){\\int }_{1}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{2}(x-1)[y]_{y=1}^{y={x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{2}(x-1)({x}^{2}-1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-x+1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =3[(4-\\frac{8}{3}-2+2)-(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+1)] \\\\  & =3(\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{5}{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4a370ffb-9f03-59b2-ac60-62d599bab917"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4a370ffb-9f03-59b2-ac60-62d599bab917" :answer-field/id #uuid "4a370ffb-9f03-59b2-ac60-62d599bab917" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d153fc31-a51b-5ff0-a56d-5400a28d1dca" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d153fc31-a51b-5ff0-a56d-5400a28d1dca" "answer-0b87866d-7ca3-5ffe-9bf1-7ca2dbed6087" "answer-0094f649-619d-53d4-bd4a-599974a79f65" "answer-88871071-0592-5a72-9ee4-9896fb71c1b1" "answer-617c7900-1787-5067-bf53-b914ea33d964"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d153fc31-a51b-5ff0-a56d-5400a28d1dca" :answer/id #uuid "d153fc31-a51b-5ff0-a56d-5400a28d1dca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b87866d-7ca3-5ffe-9bf1-7ca2dbed6087" :answer/id #uuid "0b87866d-7ca3-5ffe-9bf1-7ca2dbed6087" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{13}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0094f649-619d-53d4-bd4a-599974a79f65" :answer/id #uuid "0094f649-619d-53d4-bd4a-599974a79f65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88871071-0592-5a72-9ee4-9896fb71c1b1" :answer/id #uuid "88871071-0592-5a72-9ee4-9896fb71c1b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-617c7900-1787-5067-bf53-b914ea33d964" :answer/id #uuid "617c7900-1787-5067-bf53-b914ea33d964" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9a19ca23-6c60-5d6e-8799-38d55529cc16" :question/id #uuid "9a19ca23-6c60-5d6e-8799-38d55529cc16" :question/math-academy-id "q-143871" :question/problem "Find $\\text{E}[{(X+Y)}^{2}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4},\\, & y\\le x\\le 4-y,\\,0\\le y\\le 2; \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=(x+y)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\le x\\le 4-y,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\},$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](images/8d/8d1935944ffa0017da736fc540ff823df8a052c2c32c985553cde08ea5fce8da.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{(X+Y)}^{2}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}(x+y)^{2}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)^{2}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y}^{4-y}(x+y)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}(x+y)^{3}]}_{x=y}^{x=4-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\frac{1}{3}(4-y+y)^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}(y+y)^{3}]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({4}^{3}-(2y)^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(64-8{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{[64y-2{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(128-32) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-92fcb5de-d0cd-5bb2-911a-0615ed463dd0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-92fcb5de-d0cd-5bb2-911a-0615ed463dd0" :answer-field/id #uuid "92fcb5de-d0cd-5bb2-911a-0615ed463dd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-48289cb0-2db9-541c-ba9b-d4327439a579" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ee5a9688-f2d5-553e-872b-13b2824acaba" "answer-ce683808-6c11-54ae-bb49-f99568096871" "answer-48289cb0-2db9-541c-ba9b-d4327439a579" "answer-626f8019-c00b-5e2b-9eae-917eb16fcd3d" "answer-77cd1431-7dea-5816-88cc-589a8ce57085"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee5a9688-f2d5-553e-872b-13b2824acaba" :answer/id #uuid "ee5a9688-f2d5-553e-872b-13b2824acaba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce683808-6c11-54ae-bb49-f99568096871" :answer/id #uuid "ce683808-6c11-54ae-bb49-f99568096871" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48289cb0-2db9-541c-ba9b-d4327439a579" :answer/id #uuid "48289cb0-2db9-541c-ba9b-d4327439a579" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-626f8019-c00b-5e2b-9eae-917eb16fcd3d" :answer/id #uuid "626f8019-c00b-5e2b-9eae-917eb16fcd3d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77cd1431-7dea-5816-88cc-589a8ce57085" :answer/id #uuid "77cd1431-7dea-5816-88cc-589a8ce57085" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162118 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f125a564-9e7e-50c1-b77c-f6cbd0ae6169" "question-9ba35bb1-196e-51b8-b71e-102fe895702c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f125a564-9e7e-50c1-b77c-f6cbd0ae6169" :question/id #uuid "f125a564-9e7e-50c1-b77c-f6cbd0ae6169" :question/math-academy-id "q-143879" :question/problem "Find $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}xy,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonzero only in the rectangular region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}y+x{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{y{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{3}{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{y}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{4}}{8}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+2-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-719de611-fe3d-510b-8ec3-a7dea6d0ecc5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-719de611-fe3d-510b-8ec3-a7dea6d0ecc5" :answer-field/id #uuid "719de611-fe3d-510b-8ec3-a7dea6d0ecc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cfcead82-4acf-5200-8fd5-75bb552b7196" :answer-field/choices ["answer-67d3f414-e5a3-5a85-86ff-7be8a7d0d430" "answer-b6768082-39bc-5d53-bad6-15fd36ca45b1" "answer-58ac6aa4-3187-51f4-8986-37a77eb9556e" "answer-cfcead82-4acf-5200-8fd5-75bb552b7196" "answer-6ddc4b8b-fe6b-5cab-a0b5-82d769645de3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67d3f414-e5a3-5a85-86ff-7be8a7d0d430" :answer/id #uuid "67d3f414-e5a3-5a85-86ff-7be8a7d0d430" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6768082-39bc-5d53-bad6-15fd36ca45b1" :answer/id #uuid "b6768082-39bc-5d53-bad6-15fd36ca45b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58ac6aa4-3187-51f4-8986-37a77eb9556e" :answer/id #uuid "58ac6aa4-3187-51f4-8986-37a77eb9556e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cfcead82-4acf-5200-8fd5-75bb552b7196" :answer/id #uuid "cfcead82-4acf-5200-8fd5-75bb552b7196" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ddc4b8b-fe6b-5cab-a0b5-82d769645de3" :answer/id #uuid "6ddc4b8b-fe6b-5cab-a0b5-82d769645de3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162405 :question/problem "Find $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}(Y+1)],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\, & 1\\le x\\le 3,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)={x}^{3}(y+1).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonzero only in the rectangular region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,1\\le y\\le 2\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}(Y+1)] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}{x}^{3}(y+1)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{3}(y+1)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{3}(y+1)\\cdot \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y(y+1){\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y(y+1){[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4y(y+1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{4{y}^{3}}{3}+2{y}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & ={[\\frac{4{y}^{3}}{3}+2{y}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{32}{3}+8)-(\\frac{4}{3}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{46}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186159632 :answer-field/correct 17592186159637 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-9ba35bb1-196e-51b8-b71e-102fe895702c" :question/id #uuid "9ba35bb1-196e-51b8-b71e-102fe895702c" :question/math-academy-id "q-143884" :question/problem "Find $\\text{E}[{e}^{X-Y}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)={e}^{x-y}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonzero only in the rectangular region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{e}^{X-Y}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}{e}^{x-y}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x-y}\\cdot {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}[{e}^{x}]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(e-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[(e-1)y]_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =(e-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b806adf7-55c6-5e96-8591-27f8f3de0558"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b806adf7-55c6-5e96-8591-27f8f3de0558" :answer-field/id #uuid "b806adf7-55c6-5e96-8591-27f8f3de0558" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a5bb0ace-70a3-52eb-95ec-8d06773149b5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a5bb0ace-70a3-52eb-95ec-8d06773149b5" "answer-fda9eb6e-8342-5d4d-a8b8-51e569d33bde" "answer-419508b7-b598-5bac-aefc-71912a4034c8" "answer-b2538d50-74dd-5675-80ce-ae08eaba9106" "answer-52e29e0e-3acf-5e78-b135-92a31a0d1a0a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5bb0ace-70a3-52eb-95ec-8d06773149b5" :answer/id #uuid "a5bb0ace-70a3-52eb-95ec-8d06773149b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(e-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fda9eb6e-8342-5d4d-a8b8-51e569d33bde" :answer/id #uuid "fda9eb6e-8342-5d4d-a8b8-51e569d33bde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(e-1)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-419508b7-b598-5bac-aefc-71912a4034c8" :answer/id #uuid "419508b7-b598-5bac-aefc-71912a4034c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2538d50-74dd-5675-80ce-ae08eaba9106" :answer/id #uuid "b2538d50-74dd-5675-80ce-ae08eaba9106" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{e-1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52e29e0e-3acf-5e78-b135-92a31a0d1a0a" :answer/id #uuid "52e29e0e-3acf-5e78-b135-92a31a0d1a0a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-2cd388bd-d3e4-5270-8c71-7d4eecc1b15e" :activity/id #uuid "2cd388bd-d3e4-5270-8c71-7d4eecc1b15e" :activity/title "The Rule of the Lazy Statistician for Two Random Variables" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050368 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-45242d45-bd36-5121-80cd-9ac928d0a8f1" "step-8cf947ae-27ad-584f-9aba-1879a81641dc" "step-9e2158cb-c797-5395-8f29-3423911f68bf" "step-813ce03b-2bc9-56d9-bce8-95ab823f7304" "step-2059c6c7-dfef-5a9d-83eb-99b192520566"] :activity/first-step "step-45242d45-bd36-5121-80cd-9ac928d0a8f1" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-45242d45-bd36-5121-80cd-9ac928d0a8f1" :step/id #uuid "45242d45-bd36-5121-80cd-9ac928d0a8f1" :step/content "question-da99206a-ee96-54b9-a8bb-1c95438c5559" :step/next "step-8cf947ae-27ad-584f-9aba-1879a81641dc" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8cf947ae-27ad-584f-9aba-1879a81641dc" :step/id #uuid "8cf947ae-27ad-584f-9aba-1879a81641dc" :step/content "question-9a19ca23-6c60-5d6e-8799-38d55529cc16" :step/next "step-9e2158cb-c797-5395-8f29-3423911f68bf" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9e2158cb-c797-5395-8f29-3423911f68bf" :step/id #uuid "9e2158cb-c797-5395-8f29-3423911f68bf" :step/content "question-f125a564-9e7e-50c1-b77c-f6cbd0ae6169" :step/next "step-813ce03b-2bc9-56d9-bce8-95ab823f7304" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-813ce03b-2bc9-56d9-bce8-95ab823f7304" :step/id #uuid "813ce03b-2bc9-56d9-bce8-95ab823f7304" :step/content 17592186162405 :step/next "step-2059c6c7-dfef-5a9d-83eb-99b192520566" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2059c6c7-dfef-5a9d-83eb-99b192520566" :step/id #uuid "2059c6c7-dfef-5a9d-83eb-99b192520566" :step/content "question-9ba35bb1-196e-51b8-b71e-102fe895702c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133737 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8c77c325-2e41-51df-821c-943abffd054d" "question-9362856b-6dfd-5551-b35f-afef6a56607c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-8c77c325-2e41-51df-821c-943abffd054d" :question/id #uuid "8c77c325-2e41-51df-821c-943abffd054d" :question/math-academy-id "q-75520" :question/problem "${7.3}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to calculate the product of $7.3$ with itself:\n\n$$\n{7.3}^{2}=(7.3)\\cdot (7.3)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{2} \\\\ \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $73\\times 73=5\\,329.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($7.3$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($7.3$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $5\\,329$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n53\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{29}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${7.3}^{2}=53.29\\,.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5b277ddb-c4ed-5fd4-b2dc-c14fccceaffe"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5b277ddb-c4ed-5fd4-b2dc-c14fccceaffe" :answer-field/id #uuid "5b277ddb-c4ed-5fd4-b2dc-c14fccceaffe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fe7b2309-a94f-5c47-83f4-fd09d8bb04d5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dc6fe410-d7fb-5fa7-84e2-d245a133c254" "answer-2ff1f921-52e3-539a-b46b-8486b70c61bc" "answer-4ef14f82-1314-55e0-aa7f-d6fa02048203" "answer-fe7b2309-a94f-5c47-83f4-fd09d8bb04d5" "answer-9ce61d4c-674e-501c-94f8-21794f4cd123"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc6fe410-d7fb-5fa7-84e2-d245a133c254" :answer/id #uuid "dc6fe410-d7fb-5fa7-84e2-d245a133c254" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14.29" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ff1f921-52e3-539a-b46b-8486b70c61bc" :answer/id #uuid "2ff1f921-52e3-539a-b46b-8486b70c61bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "49.29" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ef14f82-1314-55e0-aa7f-d6fa02048203" :answer/id #uuid "4ef14f82-1314-55e0-aa7f-d6fa02048203" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "53" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe7b2309-a94f-5c47-83f4-fd09d8bb04d5" :answer/id #uuid "fe7b2309-a94f-5c47-83f4-fd09d8bb04d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "53.29" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ce61d4c-674e-501c-94f8-21794f4cd123" :answer/id #uuid "9ce61d4c-674e-501c-94f8-21794f4cd123" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "53.9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133735 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b06c7090-b9ea-594c-b431-3fe9296b347e" "question-056f7c20-573d-55c9-bb54-7bee0a1479fd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-b06c7090-b9ea-594c-b431-3fe9296b347e" :question/id #uuid "b06c7090-b9ea-594c-b431-3fe9296b347e" :question/math-academy-id "q-99437" :question/problem "${(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "To work out \n${(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2},$\n we multiply \n$\\frac{1}{9}$\n by itself:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}=\\frac{1}{81}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-695816b9-028c-5cdf-b7e1-17fd412268d1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-695816b9-028c-5cdf-b7e1-17fd412268d1" :answer-field/id #uuid "695816b9-028c-5cdf-b7e1-17fd412268d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ffd5b701-3c55-5a13-9705-5e37e7b904b8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6431f8e0-22c1-5a92-b706-ab325a5dfa0d" "answer-274904f4-58f7-56e2-9b6d-31c1ae57ce78" "answer-ffd5b701-3c55-5a13-9705-5e37e7b904b8" "answer-8776b217-a7c6-5847-98f1-2b8609aea8cd" "answer-a6f1ac2b-edcc-5f11-8220-b6a757312e57"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6431f8e0-22c1-5a92-b706-ab325a5dfa0d" :answer/id #uuid "6431f8e0-22c1-5a92-b706-ab325a5dfa0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-274904f4-58f7-56e2-9b6d-31c1ae57ce78" :answer/id #uuid "274904f4-58f7-56e2-9b6d-31c1ae57ce78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{49}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffd5b701-3c55-5a13-9705-5e37e7b904b8" :answer/id #uuid "ffd5b701-3c55-5a13-9705-5e37e7b904b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{81}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8776b217-a7c6-5847-98f1-2b8609aea8cd" :answer/id #uuid "8776b217-a7c6-5847-98f1-2b8609aea8cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{90}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6f1ac2b-edcc-5f11-8220-b6a757312e57" :answer/id #uuid "a6f1ac2b-edcc-5f11-8220-b6a757312e57" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3cfc1cc8-75ac-58cb-baba-115d9b2db8af" :question/id #uuid "3cfc1cc8-75ac-58cb-baba-115d9b2db8af" :question/math-academy-id "q-185181" :question/problem "Expressed as an improper fraction or mixed number, ${(-\\frac{8}{5})}^{2}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To work out ${(-\\frac{8}{5})}^{2},$ we multiply $-\\frac{8}{5}$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-\\frac{8}{5})}^{2} & = \\\\ (-\\frac{8}{5})\\cdot (-\\frac{8}{5}) & = \\\\ \\frac{8\\cdot 8}{5\\cdot 5} & = \\\\ \\frac{64}{25} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dfa2a1c0-d3ce-5d21-872c-ede324a37869"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dfa2a1c0-d3ce-5d21-872c-ede324a37869" :answer-field/id #uuid "dfa2a1c0-d3ce-5d21-872c-ede324a37869" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-cb37b488-9bf2-5a55-9b98-ff72718291b6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cb37b488-9bf2-5a55-9b98-ff72718291b6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cb37b488-9bf2-5a55-9b98-ff72718291b6" :answer/id #uuid "cb37b488-9bf2-5a55-9b98-ff72718291b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{64}{25}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-056f7c20-573d-55c9-bb54-7bee0a1479fd" :question/id #uuid "056f7c20-573d-55c9-bb54-7bee0a1479fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-185189" :question/problem "Expressed as an improper fraction or mixed number, ${(\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To work out ${(\\frac{5}{3})}^{2},$ we multiply $\\frac{5}{3}$ by itself:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}=\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{3}=\\frac{25}{9}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6d582431-5271-591c-a589-e2a2aea28272"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6d582431-5271-591c-a589-e2a2aea28272" :answer-field/id #uuid "6d582431-5271-591c-a589-e2a2aea28272" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-540b5a5a-5141-58c1-9bbe-df63004cb07d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-540b5a5a-5141-58c1-9bbe-df63004cb07d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-540b5a5a-5141-58c1-9bbe-df63004cb07d" :answer/id #uuid "540b5a5a-5141-58c1-9bbe-df63004cb07d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{25}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9362856b-6dfd-5551-b35f-afef6a56607c" :question/id #uuid "9362856b-6dfd-5551-b35f-afef6a56607c" :question/math-academy-id "q-185205" :question/problem "Expressed as a decimal in its simplest form, $(-4.5)^{2}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We need to calculate the product of $(-4.5)$ with itself:\n\n$$\n(-4.5)^{2}=(-4.5)\\cdot (-4.5)=(4.5)\\cdot (4.5)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{2} \\\\ \\boxed{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $45\\times 45=2\\,025.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($4.5$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($4.5$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $2\\,025$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n20\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{25}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(-4.5)^{2}=20.25\\,.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-80f35c7a-0c9b-50f3-b115-b30ce556b18b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-80f35c7a-0c9b-50f3-b115-b30ce556b18b" :answer-field/id #uuid "80f35c7a-0c9b-50f3-b115-b30ce556b18b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f2c06f3e-ac54-53ac-853c-0a9d19f1a9ad" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f2c06f3e-ac54-53ac-853c-0a9d19f1a9ad"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2c06f3e-ac54-53ac-853c-0a9d19f1a9ad" :answer/id #uuid "f2c06f3e-ac54-53ac-853c-0a9d19f1a9ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-a5e6c4aa-e33a-5632-9ea3-6d08ffe1753f" :activity/id #uuid "a5e6c4aa-e33a-5632-9ea3-6d08ffe1753f" :activity/title "Squaring Rational Numbers" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050428 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-d2bbd8f7-e12b-5835-805d-1d67c253c50f" "step-2a01f71d-97d1-5207-b8b2-d2e65ddf34ab" "step-2ca76ade-a0fe-5c1b-bf99-b23c6a48abbb" "step-a725bc28-e1db-5604-962d-8da46d9083f2" "step-f52bc408-bc3b-523d-a977-71987b08adcc"] :activity/first-step "step-d2bbd8f7-e12b-5835-805d-1d67c253c50f" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-d2bbd8f7-e12b-5835-805d-1d67c253c50f" :step/id #uuid "d2bbd8f7-e12b-5835-805d-1d67c253c50f" :step/content "question-8c77c325-2e41-51df-821c-943abffd054d" :step/next "step-2a01f71d-97d1-5207-b8b2-d2e65ddf34ab" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2a01f71d-97d1-5207-b8b2-d2e65ddf34ab" :step/id #uuid "2a01f71d-97d1-5207-b8b2-d2e65ddf34ab" :step/content "question-b06c7090-b9ea-594c-b431-3fe9296b347e" :step/next "step-2ca76ade-a0fe-5c1b-bf99-b23c6a48abbb" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2ca76ade-a0fe-5c1b-bf99-b23c6a48abbb" :step/id #uuid "2ca76ade-a0fe-5c1b-bf99-b23c6a48abbb" :step/content "question-3cfc1cc8-75ac-58cb-baba-115d9b2db8af" :step/next "step-a725bc28-e1db-5604-962d-8da46d9083f2" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a725bc28-e1db-5604-962d-8da46d9083f2" :step/id #uuid "a725bc28-e1db-5604-962d-8da46d9083f2" :step/content "question-056f7c20-573d-55c9-bb54-7bee0a1479fd" :step/next "step-f52bc408-bc3b-523d-a977-71987b08adcc" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f52bc408-bc3b-523d-a977-71987b08adcc" :step/id #uuid "f52bc408-bc3b-523d-a977-71987b08adcc" :step/content "question-9362856b-6dfd-5551-b35f-afef6a56607c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107127 :tutorial/content "When $C$ is a closed curve, there is always a well-defined notion of \"inside\" and \"outside\" $C.$ It is usually easy to determine which region is inside or outside an arbitrary closed curve using our natural intuition.\n\nOne important quantity that we often need when dealing with closed, oriented curves is the **outward-pointing unit normal vector,** usually denoted $\\mathbf n$.\n\nAs the name suggests:\n\n- $\\mathbf n$ is normal (i.e., perpendicular) to the curve $C$ at *every* point,\n\n- it is a unit vector (i.e., its magnitude equals $1$), and\n\n- it always points in the outward direction.\n\nSince $\\mathbf n$ must be normal to $C$ at *every* point, it must vary as we move around $C.$ Therefore, it is usually written either as a function of the parameter space (i.e., $\\mathbf n(t)$) or as a function of the coordinates of the points on $C$ (i.e., $\\mathbf n(x,y)$). \n\nAs a concrete example, let's consider the closed, positively oriented ellipse $C,$ parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = 3\\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 2\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\n![](images/fe/fea4c20453e9c80493f5c5431859fd7918d75cbaa494ad0b3deeccacfae24019.png)\n\nFor convenience, we first express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n![](images/c7/c7d514dbfdb746d94487a7ebca6b239173b854010eeccb80b4afc06cd09a9c0e.svg)\n\nLet's calculate the vector $\\mathbf n$ for this curve. To do this, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA typical tangent vector for our curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/10/10ae867f682158bce2098234210206d4d7eb811be780505ca1984fb9e9516a27.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n![](images/27/27e11f86245d4331e33cec34742a7b6c395ff22fe95bcf465ec569f7168b4c0f.svg)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get the vector $\\tilde{\\mathbf n}(t),$ given by\n\n![](images/00/006458079641036b283371fb36a94b39b11dcb09d796175a555539287218d8a7.svg)\n\nThe vector $\\tilde{\\mathbf n}(t)$ is shown below:\n\n![](images/7c/7ca9a18d78783a923b63fcc41a8bc41ccab5c4a12bb00f5c305e35e2e1bc40e5.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nThe vector $\\tilde{\\mathbf n}(t)$ in an outward-pointing normal vector, but it is not a unit vector. Therefore, we need to normalize it.\n\nNotice that  \n\n$$\n||\\mathbf r'(t)|| = \\sqrt{(3\\sin t)^2 + (2\\cos t)^2} = \\sqrt{9\\sin^2 t + 4\\cos^2 t}.\n$$\n\nFurthermore, the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore, we can calculate our outward-pointing unit vector $\\mathbf n$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}(t)}{||{r}^{′}(t)||} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{2\\cos t\\,\\mathbf i + 3\\sin t\\,\\mathbf j}{\\sqrt{9\\sin^2 t + 4\\cos^2 t}}\n$$\n\ngives us an outward-pointing unit normal vector to every point on our ellipse." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107129 :tutorial/content "Let's consider a general, closed curve $C$ in the plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = x(t)\\,\\mathbf{i} + y(t)\\,\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nWe will assume that $C$ is oriented counterclockwise. Our goal is to derive a general formula for $\\mathbf n.$ \n\nAs always, we first express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n![](images/48/48517c1b1d9915c8f739f8a3979778eb7ea8ddebe01f276034f8df1c301ce802.svg)\n\nTo calculate $\\mathbf n,$ we follow our usual three steps.\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n![](images/49/492e23c319bc0681cf1c65d0ff91fccdde02686891b3ae1c1daf9f512f38a7e7.svg)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get the vector \n![](images/e4/e44acf1d3bdb1caaf25b89faa191a58faa7979881d9a7093ec23ea9ab8405103.svg)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNotice that  \n\n$$\n||\\mathbf r'(t)|| = \\sqrt{(x'(t))^2 + (y'(t))^2}.\n$$\n\nFurthermore, the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore, we can calculate our outward-pointing unit vector $\\mathbf n$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) \\\\ -{x}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\,i-\\frac{{x}^{′}(t)}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)\\,i-{x}^{′}(t)\\,j}{\\sqrt{({x}^{′}(t))^{2}+({y}^{′}(t))^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id "question-0a27d18e-df05-520d-948c-41b1732464a2" :question/id #uuid "0a27d18e-df05-520d-948c-41b1732464a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-60606" :question/problem "![](images/84/84da1d1bb056c8c5d823a585889699e3ba3fe5ca7b2706b4e0a16c96f2e65ac3.png)\n\nWhat is the distance between the points $(3,1)$  and $(3,4)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1** \n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $3$ units, so the distance equals $3.$\n\n![](images/a0/a0eb36fd37d73ed20e0ee17717f80cb33a56910cc57eb181897ad44197a7f268.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](images/2b/2b4771270aec2dd62828f4063262cfaf81fba31f6be6d1a2ba8bfe0d59778b48.png)\n\nThe point $(3,4)$ is at a distance of $4$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(3,1)$ is at a distance of $1$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $4-1=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6c47ba7d-aa0c-5abb-a75b-b6921e6c2540"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6c47ba7d-aa0c-5abb-a75b-b6921e6c2540" :answer-field/id #uuid "6c47ba7d-aa0c-5abb-a75b-b6921e6c2540" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0cc1dfda-0971-51d2-b2f6-fd8adfa0f1ba" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cf7ea040-309d-5faf-bab9-81177579d7ab" "answer-02dbb7c8-1405-51e2-be89-1394776caad6" "answer-0cc1dfda-0971-51d2-b2f6-fd8adfa0f1ba" "answer-24e265ed-ab68-54e6-95e4-cfa966d948c8" "answer-d456bc45-475c-5809-ba80-4e99f3a8ac38"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf7ea040-309d-5faf-bab9-81177579d7ab" :answer/id #uuid "cf7ea040-309d-5faf-bab9-81177579d7ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02dbb7c8-1405-51e2-be89-1394776caad6" :answer/id #uuid "02dbb7c8-1405-51e2-be89-1394776caad6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0cc1dfda-0971-51d2-b2f6-fd8adfa0f1ba" :answer/id #uuid "0cc1dfda-0971-51d2-b2f6-fd8adfa0f1ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24e265ed-ab68-54e6-95e4-cfa966d948c8" :answer/id #uuid "24e265ed-ab68-54e6-95e4-cfa966d948c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d456bc45-475c-5809-ba80-4e99f3a8ac38" :answer/id #uuid "d456bc45-475c-5809-ba80-4e99f3a8ac38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073900 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-67699b33-3e46-5c7c-ad2b-2f6df6bd8eb1" "question-3ab19b4a-4627-54ee-8e9e-8d051f6ac141"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-67699b33-3e46-5c7c-ad2b-2f6df6bd8eb1" :question/id #uuid "67699b33-3e46-5c7c-ad2b-2f6df6bd8eb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-61105" :question/problem "![](images/25/25e1891ce31fa7043723b2dcb4cd1e01c41dbd82ae70ef5024c64ed2d6deab33.png)\n\nWhat is the distance between the points $(-3,1)$  and $(-3,-5)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1** \n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $6$ units, so the distance equals $6.$\n\n![](images/1f/1f3eeede9aa301cf6d64b48cc86f770fc86517ed51285da9bdc86e5a580b243f.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then adding.\n\n![](images/a5/a504ca22769a377cd7e23e0b6a09595d00c8ad25733f3d1e6a716b52f262e139.png)\n\nThe point $(-3,-5)$ is at a distance of $5$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(-3,1)$ is at a distance of $1$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $5+1=6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7a86d0ab-34e6-5c19-97fc-00a0a1fc39ad"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7a86d0ab-34e6-5c19-97fc-00a0a1fc39ad" :answer-field/id #uuid "7a86d0ab-34e6-5c19-97fc-00a0a1fc39ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-199f5bd1-cf24-5ec5-87fb-e715dfad0524" :answer-field/choices ["answer-921e2d09-f58f-50dc-91f3-e6324b399fae" "answer-7e700299-d0cc-5e63-95d1-97a04cb6ea9a" "answer-9dd79885-eb4b-5b18-8642-27db39ab655a" "answer-199f5bd1-cf24-5ec5-87fb-e715dfad0524" "answer-4446140c-ebf8-5421-a61b-bfae02059671"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-921e2d09-f58f-50dc-91f3-e6324b399fae" :answer/id #uuid "921e2d09-f58f-50dc-91f3-e6324b399fae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e700299-d0cc-5e63-95d1-97a04cb6ea9a" :answer/id #uuid "7e700299-d0cc-5e63-95d1-97a04cb6ea9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9dd79885-eb4b-5b18-8642-27db39ab655a" :answer/id #uuid "9dd79885-eb4b-5b18-8642-27db39ab655a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-199f5bd1-cf24-5ec5-87fb-e715dfad0524" :answer/id #uuid "199f5bd1-cf24-5ec5-87fb-e715dfad0524" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4446140c-ebf8-5421-a61b-bfae02059671" :answer/id #uuid "4446140c-ebf8-5421-a61b-bfae02059671" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186074465 :question/problem "What is the distance between the points $(4,2)$  and $(4,8)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/66/66c988b7fede0e97eae46388dd0e57aee2c49a3c185c9a888277da8fefc947ab.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $6$ units, so the distance equals $6.$\n\n![](images/63/63f70c20c918dae51ecddc7891a8cbee7c5749720521e62fe26e9bc4e33bb9e6.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](images/d3/d3693b8c3d07636143b2c77c3bd2645015885b77ec4e8ddffde2e121da0511bc.png)\n\nThe point $(4,8)$ is at a distance of $8$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(4,2)$ is at a distance of $2$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $8-2=6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073028 :answer-field/correct 17592186073031 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-3ab19b4a-4627-54ee-8e9e-8d051f6ac141" :question/id #uuid "3ab19b4a-4627-54ee-8e9e-8d051f6ac141" :question/math-academy-id "q-61265" :question/problem "What is the distance between the points $(-3,2)$  and $(-3,-4)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/c0/c0dcef8b6c0c63e17fc9b516e3e3176f054dd0eddb93c71375a3de3272da9623.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $6$ units, so the distance equals $6.$\n\n![](images/c1/c1764499f4600d5c74c598b205e0a1971ca91231c4464f77e0809a680003bf11.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then adding.\n\n![](images/5b/5b4c4c665b07575604dc873703517548404d7c31e8395070642ca37584c6d08f.png)\n\nThe point $(-3,2)$ is at a distance of $2$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(-3,-4)$ is at a distance of $4$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $4+2=6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ba2c0b6c-0868-582f-9fb0-8f7239a4a7d0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ba2c0b6c-0868-582f-9fb0-8f7239a4a7d0" :answer-field/id #uuid "ba2c0b6c-0868-582f-9fb0-8f7239a4a7d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-068e3080-4337-581d-9b8b-cc0e663f7828" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b70f45a0-ba84-55de-ab9d-38674206db65" "answer-21bbce83-c3c6-5f3b-ac9d-a19684c5d630" "answer-155fbdce-2870-53b4-bdf7-758d24346ab9" "answer-0a1c255d-9fb4-5411-b2aa-255dce8090e4" "answer-068e3080-4337-581d-9b8b-cc0e663f7828"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b70f45a0-ba84-55de-ab9d-38674206db65" :answer/id #uuid "b70f45a0-ba84-55de-ab9d-38674206db65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21bbce83-c3c6-5f3b-ac9d-a19684c5d630" :answer/id #uuid "21bbce83-c3c6-5f3b-ac9d-a19684c5d630" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-155fbdce-2870-53b4-bdf7-758d24346ab9" :answer/id #uuid "155fbdce-2870-53b4-bdf7-758d24346ab9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a1c255d-9fb4-5411-b2aa-255dce8090e4" :answer/id #uuid "0a1c255d-9fb4-5411-b2aa-255dce8090e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-068e3080-4337-581d-9b8b-cc0e663f7828" :answer/id #uuid "068e3080-4337-581d-9b8b-cc0e663f7828" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-6400b63d-ece5-55c3-ace8-f3cf09eb034e" :activity/id #uuid "6400b63d-ece5-55c3-ace8-f3cf09eb034e" :activity/title "Distances Between Points in the Coordinate Plane" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049868 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-12252c44-f6ed-5a4c-877a-529ad585fdea" "step-5c465ab7-ebb0-5979-8fd6-300e20ddd25b" "step-f27b2fa2-b4cf-5259-af6d-f395c41888da" "step-35f258ab-51ea-5cc4-ab06-dadec569e07a"] :activity/first-step "step-12252c44-f6ed-5a4c-877a-529ad585fdea" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-12252c44-f6ed-5a4c-877a-529ad585fdea" :step/id #uuid "12252c44-f6ed-5a4c-877a-529ad585fdea" :step/content "question-0a27d18e-df05-520d-948c-41b1732464a2" :step/next "step-5c465ab7-ebb0-5979-8fd6-300e20ddd25b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5c465ab7-ebb0-5979-8fd6-300e20ddd25b" :step/id #uuid "5c465ab7-ebb0-5979-8fd6-300e20ddd25b" :step/content "question-67699b33-3e46-5c7c-ad2b-2f6df6bd8eb1" :step/next "step-f27b2fa2-b4cf-5259-af6d-f395c41888da" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f27b2fa2-b4cf-5259-af6d-f395c41888da" :step/id #uuid "f27b2fa2-b4cf-5259-af6d-f395c41888da" :step/content 17592186074465 :step/next "step-35f258ab-51ea-5cc4-ab06-dadec569e07a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-35f258ab-51ea-5cc4-ab06-dadec569e07a" :step/id #uuid "35f258ab-51ea-5cc4-ab06-dadec569e07a" :step/content "question-3ab19b4a-4627-54ee-8e9e-8d051f6ac141" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068744 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d6fb18a1-0afa-52ee-a564-35b978a84f8b" "question-246f9007-c107-5fa6-a212-74a6a9a70dc6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068577 :question/problem "Factor $x^3 - 8.$" :question/worked-solution "The difference of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\na^3-b^3=(a-b)(a^2+ab+b^2).\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the given expression as a difference of cubes:\n\n$$\nx^3 - 8 = x^3 - 2^3\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the given expression using the difference of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nx^3 - 8 & = x^3 - 2^3\\[3pt]\n\n& = (x - 2)\\left(x^2 + 2\\cdot x + 2^2\\right)\\[3pt]\n\n& = (x - 2)\\left(x^2 + 2x + 4\\right)\t\t\n\t}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068745 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-36ce6c6d-22d9-5124-bf0e-5ddb030e27ef" "question-0184b3b4-f2c2-5b99-95bf-75c75b254d85"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068578 :question/problem "Factor $8n^3 + m^6.$" :question/worked-solution "The sum of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\na^3+b^3=(a+b)(a^2-ab+b^2).\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write our expression as a sum of cubes, as follows:\n\n$$\n8n^3 + m^6 = (2n)^3 + (m^2)^3\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the sum of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{n}^{3}+{m}^{6} & =(2n)^{3}+({m}^{2})^{3} \\\\  & =(2n+{m}^{2})((2n)^{2}-2n\\cdot {m}^{2}+({m}^{2})^{2}) \\\\  & =(2n+{m}^{2})(4{n}^{2}-2{m}^{2}n+{m}^{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068746 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-efa83c70-ebda-58c7-9dc4-66acbcb8874b" "question-ee3cc387-c8ba-5e21-b6bf-54f77512a2c2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068579 :question/problem "Factor $1 - \\dfrac 1 {t^9}.$" :question/worked-solution "The difference of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\na^3-b^3=(a-b)(a^2+ab+b^2).\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of cubes, as follows:\n\n$$\n1 - \\dfrac{1}{t^9}  = 1^3 - \\left( \\dfrac{1}{t^3}\\right)^3\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{9}} & ={1}^{3}-{(\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}})}^{3} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}})({1}^{2}+1\\cdot \\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+{(\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}})}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}})(1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{({t}^{3})^{2}}) \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}})(1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{6}})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-d6fb18a1-0afa-52ee-a564-35b978a84f8b" :question/id #uuid "d6fb18a1-0afa-52ee-a564-35b978a84f8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-3601" :question/problem "Factor $1-{j}^{3/2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The difference of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}-{b}^{3}=(a-b)({a}^{2}+ab+{b}^{2}).\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write our expression as a difference of cubes:\n\n$$\n1-{j}^{3/2}={1}^{3}-{({j}^{1/2})}^{3}={1}^{3}-{(\\sqrt{j})}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the given expression using the difference of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{j}^{3/2} & ={1}^{3}-{(\\sqrt{j})}^{3} \\\\  & =(1-\\sqrt{j})({1}^{2}+1\\cdot \\sqrt{j}+(\\sqrt{j})^{2}) \\\\  & =(1-\\sqrt{j})(1+\\sqrt{j}+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fda84225-aba2-5045-8a04-e66c3a2630f6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fda84225-aba2-5045-8a04-e66c3a2630f6" :answer-field/id #uuid "fda84225-aba2-5045-8a04-e66c3a2630f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-064d15c3-a0de-5346-b883-d6b90e56b38b" :answer-